/* ===== FUENTES: 57 registros extraídos de compare-table-con_tablas.csv =====
   Campos: n, doi, authors, year, journal, title, abstract, methods, results,
   conclusions, publisher, open, insight (síntesis en español) y tag. ===== */
const SOURCES = [{"n":1,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.accinf.2024.100695","authors":"Song, J.M.; Wang, T.; Yen, J.-C.; Chen, Y.-H.","year":"2024","journal":"International Journal of Accounting Information Systems","title":"Does cybersecurity maturity level assurance improve cybersecurity risk management in supply chains?","abstract":"This study uses analytical models to investigate whether requiring cybersecurity assurance or a particular maturity level for vendors or contractors will help them improve their cybersecurity management. Our findings suggest that, if a supplier decides on its preferred cybersecurity maturity level without knowing what level a contract requires, the supplier is more likely to exert more effort to improve its cybersecurity management. We also show that a buyer can incentivize the supplier to engage in improving cybersecurity risk management by imposing a reduced contractual price or a fine when a breach occurs. Our findings reveal the role played by cybersecurity maturity level assurance and we discuss practical implications.","methods":"Analytical game-theory model with two sequential games between a buyer and supplier, examining cybersecurity assessment, security effort, and perfunctory effort under alternative decision orders.","results":"When the buyer sets cybersecurity requirements first, the strategy can backfire: suppliers may increase perfunctory effort instead of security effort, especially under certain marginal-cost conditions. The backfire is less likely when suppliers choose maturity levels before knowing contract requirements; lower profit margins or breach penalties also reduce backfire.","conclusions":"Requiring cybersecurity assurance may not improve supply-chain cybersecurity if suppliers can respond after seeing the requirement; choosing suppliers with preexisting maturity levels is more effective. Limitations include model assumptions, no archival data for empirical verification, and the need for future empirical, psychological, and multi-period studies.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":false,"insight":"Elegir proveedores con madurez previa supera a imponer requisitos después: las exigencias tardías pueden producir esfuerzo aparente en lugar de seguridad real.","tag":"Proveedores"},{"n":2,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-12-803843-7.00025-9","authors":"Harmening, Jim","year":"2017","journal":"Computer and Information Security Handbook","title":"Security Management Systems","abstract":"Today, when most companies and government agencies rely on computer networks to store and manage their organizations' data, it is essential that measures be put in place to secure those networks and keep them functioning optimally. Network administrators need to define their security management systems to cover all parts of their computer and network resources. With a plethora of storage devices, including iPads, Android tablets, universal serial bus drives, watches, televisions, digital video recorders, smartphones, Apple TV appliances, Xboxes, and household appliances, management becomes even more difficult. This chapter focuses on security management systems, which are sets of policies put into place by an organization to maintain the security of its computer and network resources. These policies are based on the types of resources that need to be secured, and they depend on the organization. Some groups of policies can be applied to entire industries; others are specific to an individual organization. A security management system starts as a set of policies that dictate the way in which computer resources can be used. The policies are then implemented by the organization's technical departments and enforced. This can be easy for smaller organizations, but it can require a team for larger international organizations that have thousands of business processes. Either way, measures need to be put in place to prevent, respond to, and fix security issues that arise in an organization. Standards that were in place 10 years ago may not be adequate for current business practices. As an example, a strong password may have included fewer than 8 characters, whereas modern computers can easily break the password using brute force or rainbow table methods.","methods":"Book chapter/overview of security management systems. It presents standards, policies, roles, controls, risk assessment, and incident response as a conceptual framework; no formal research method is described.","results":"ISO/IEC-27001 and the SANS 20 Critical Security Controls are presented as key standards; FISMA requires government agencies to develop security management systems and annual compliance inspections. The chapter also outlines Plan-Do-Check-Act, ACLs, risk assessment items, and incident-response steps.","conclusions":"Security management systems should be built from documented policies, risk assessment, access control, training, and regular review using a Plan-Do-Act-Check cycle. The chapter warns that standards must be updated over time as technologies and threats change.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":false,"insight":"Un SGSI se construye con políticas documentadas, evaluación de riesgos, control de accesos, formación y revisión mediante el ciclo PDCA.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":3,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cose.2019.101656","authors":"Schmitz, C.; Pape, S.","year":"2020","journal":"Computers and Security","title":"LiSRA: Lightweight Security Risk Assessment for decision support in information security","abstract":"Information security risk assessment frameworks support decision-makers in assessing and understanding the risks their organisation is exposed to. However, there is a lack of lightweight approaches. Most existing frameworks require security-related information that are not available and that are very challenging to gather. So they are not suitable in practice, especially for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) who often lack in data and in security knowledge. On the other hand, other explicit SME approaches have far less informative value than LiSRA. Moreover, many approaches only provide extensive process descriptions that are challenging for SMEs. In order to overcome this challenge, we propose LiSRA, a lightweight, domain-specific framework to support information security decision-making. It is designed with a two-sided input where domain experts initially provide domain-specific information (e.g. attack scenarios for a specific domain), whereupon users can focus on specifying their security practices and organisational characteristics by entering information that many organisations have already collected. This information is then linked to attack paths and to the corresponding adverse impacts in order to finally assess the total risk. Moreover, LiSRA can be used to get transparent recommendations for future security activities and presents detailed insights on the mitigating effects of each recommendation. They are being evaluated taking into account the security activities already in place, and also considering the dependencies between multiple overlapping activities that can be of complementary, substitutive or dependent nature. Both aspects are ignored by most existing evaluation approaches which can lead to an over-investment in security. A prototype has been implemented, and the applicability of the framework has been evaluated with performance and robustness analyses and with initial qualitative evaluations.","methods":"LiSRA is a lightweight, domain-specific security risk assessment framework with four phases: expert input, user input, risk computation, and recommender application. It uses attack-control trees, COBIT maturity levels, and an attacker model to compute scenario and total risk.","results":"A prototype was implemented and evaluated with performance and robustness analyses plus initial qualitative evaluations. For 500 scenarios, median risk computation took about 0.225 seconds; tree transformations showed similar medians (103.4 ms vs 103.9 ms).","conclusions":"LiSRA is presented as a practical lightweight framework for SMEs that supports risk assessment and ranks effective, cost-efficient security activities while accounting for dependencies among controls. Limitations include one-shot attacker strategies and SME-oriented maturity modeling; future work includes budget-aware recommendations and real-world case studies.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":false,"insight":"Un análisis de riesgo ligero y por escenarios permite priorizar actividades según su efecto mitigador y las dependencias entre controles.","tag":"Riesgo"},{"n":4,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-323-90802-3.00016-2","authors":"Parker, Mitchell","year":"2023","journal":"Health Information Exchange","title":"Managing threats to health data and information: toward security","abstract":"More than 2000 ransomware and malware attacks occurred in the United States during the first half of 2021. Many of these attacks targeted health systems and healthcare providers, making cybersecurity a top priority for the health industry. Health Information Exchange (HIE) networks face more complex security challenges than healthcare providers, because HIE networks serve as an interface between multiple health system actors, such as hospitals, pharmacies, and primary care doctors. Larger volumes of data could potentially make them bigger targets for cyber-criminals. HIE networks can further help healthcare facilities affected by cyberattacks by providing access to critical data. This means that HIE networks now play a more critical role than ever in ensuring that clinicians have access to the information they need to make decisions. The purpose of this chapter is to introduce information on cybersecurity as it relates to HIE networks. It also discusses several security frameworks, approaches, risk assessments, risk management plans, available resources, and emerging trends in HIE security. The end goal is to provide the reader with sufficient information to improve the security of their organization and serve as a reliable resource in an environment made uncertain by cyberattacks.","methods":"Book chapter/review-style overview of cybersecurity for health information exchanges, organized around regulations, security frameworks, technical risk methods, risk management, and emerging trends. No formal study design or search strategy is described.","results":"Synthesizes that HIEs face heightened cybersecurity risk and need layered controls: HIPAA/HITECH, Cures Act, NIST, ISO/IEC 27001, GDPR, OCTAVE/FORTE, vulnerability scanning, OWASP, CIS Controls, and penetration testing. Also highlights ransomware, API/FHIR exposure, and the need for continual risk assessment and mitigation.","conclusions":"HIE cybersecurity is under-addressed by provider-focused guidance, so organizations need HIE-specific risk management and technical controls. A key limitation is that many frameworks do not fully cover the technical and cross-organizational risks HIEs face.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":false,"insight":"Los intercambios de información sanitaria necesitan controles por capas y evaluación continua frente a ransomware y APIs expuestas.","tag":"Salud"},{"n":5,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-443-13223-0.00001-1","authors":"Pooley, James","year":"2025","journal":"Computer and Information Security Handbook","title":"Information Security in the Modern Enterprise","abstract":"This chapter provides a set of procedures and controls for conducting assessments of information security in the modern enterprise. The procedures are customizable and can be easily tailored to provide organizations with the needed flexibility to conduct security control assessments and privacy control assessments that support organizational risk management processes and that are aligned with the stated risk tolerance of the organization. Information on building effective security assessment plans and privacy assessment plans is also provided along with guidance on analyzing assessment results.","methods":"Book chapter overview of information security in the modern enterprise; no formal empirical method or search approach is described. It organizes the discussion around assessment and planning, policies and procedures, and training, drawing on standards such as ISO/IEC 27001 and the NIST Framework.","results":"Identifies major security challenges: dynamic threats, systems designed for performance not security, fragmented endpoints, third-party access, and user resistance. Recommends risk assessment, multidisciplinary management, standards-based planning, and continuous training.","conclusions":"Information security is primarily a risk-management and management problem, not just a technology problem, and effective programs require broad organizational participation. Key caveat: there is no perfect security, and protections must be tailored to the organization’s risk environment and continuously updated.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":false,"insight":"La seguridad es principalmente un problema de gestión de riesgo y de personas, no solo de tecnología; no existe seguridad perfecta.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":6,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-443-13223-0.00025-4","authors":"Harmening, Jim","year":"2025","journal":"Computer and Information Security Handbook","title":"Security Management Systems","abstract":"Today, when most companies and government agencies rely on computer networks to store and manage their organizations' data, it is essential that measures be put in place to secure those networks and keep them functioning optimally. Network administrators need to define their security management systems to cover all parts of their computer and network resources. With a plethora of storage devices, including iPads, Android tablets, universal serial bus drives, watches, televisions, digital video recorders, smartphones, Apple TV appliances, Xboxes, and household appliances, management becomes even more difficult. This chapter focuses on security management systems, which are sets of policies put into place by an organization to maintain the security of its computer and network resources. These policies are based on the types of resources that need to be secured, and they depend on the organization. Some groups of policies can be applied to entire industries; others are specific to an individual organization. A security management system starts as a set of policies that dictate the way in which computer resources can be used. The policies are then implemented by the organization's technical departments and enforced. This can be easy for smaller organizations, but it can require a team for larger international organizations that have thousands of business processes. Either way, measures need to be put in place to prevent, respond to, and fix security issues that arise in an organization. Standards that were in place 10 years ago may not be adequate for current business practices. As an example, a strong password may have included fewer than 8 characters, whereas modern computers can easily break the password using brute force or rainbow table methods.","methods":"Book chapter overview of security management systems drawing on ISO/IEC 27001, FISMA, CIS Controls, and related frameworks; no formal research method or search strategy is described.","results":"The chapter synthesizes key practices for securing organizations: document assets, apply policies and roles, use physical/technical/administrative controls, perform risk assessment, and maintain incident-response logging and forensics.","conclusions":"A comprehensive security management system should follow a roadmap such as ISO, NIST CSF, or CISECURITY and use Plan–Do–Check–Act with annual audits. The chapter cautions that controls must be updated for new technologies and threats.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":false,"insight":"El ciclo PDCA con auditorías anuales mantiene vigente el SGSI frente a nuevas tecnologías y amenazas.","tag":"Mejora"},{"n":7,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clsr.2024.105987","authors":"Boggini, C.","year":"2024","journal":"Computer Law and Security Review","title":"Reporting cybersecurity to stakeholders: A review of CSRD and the EU cyber legal framework","abstract":"The purpose of this article is to explain, through a doctrinal review of the EU sustainability and cybersecurity legal framework, how the cybersecurity obligations contribute to the cybersecurity content and quality of the sustainability reporting. Previous studies are limited to voluntary cybersecurity disclosure in annual reports because they date back to before the adoption of CSRD. The CSRD harmonized sustainability reporting in the EU and introduced the ESRS, the standards for sustainability disclosure. As stated in the ESRS S4, the sustainability reporting should now address how the company manages the risks linked to data usage and data collection. Therefore, cybersecurity measures must be included in the sustainability report. This information can be used by stakeholders to assess the risk appetite and the potential long-term profitability of the company. The cybersecurity measures adopted by companies must comply with the cybersecurity obligations of the EU legal framework. While it is out of the scope of these cybersecurity obligations to inform ex ante the stakeholders of a company of how the company is managing cyber risks, these same obligations can improve the quality and content of the sustainability discourse.","methods":"Doctrinal review of the EU sustainability and cybersecurity legal framework, focused on CSRD, ESRS S4, GDPR, and NIS 2 for large listed EU companies.","results":"The paper argues that CSRD and ESRS S4 make cybersecurity disclosure part of sustainability reporting, and that GDPR Article 32 and NIS 2 Article 21/20 improve the content and quality of cyber information in annual reports.","conclusions":"Cybersecurity disclosure in sustainability reports should improve under CSRD because mandatory EU cyber rules align with ESRS S4. Limitation: the paper is doctrinal and does not test reporting effects empirically.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":true,"insight":"El RGPD y NIS2 elevan la calidad de la divulgación de ciberseguridad; el reporte de sostenibilidad ya incorpora los riesgos digitales.","tag":"Legal"},{"n":8,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1108/17542731111139455","authors":"Gillies, A.","year":"2011","journal":"The TQM Journal","title":"Improving the quality of information security management systems with ISO27000","abstract":"The ISO27001 standard provides a model for “establishing, implementing, operating, monitoring, reviewing, maintaining and improving an Information Security Management System (ISMS)”. This paper seeks to consider the global adoption of the ISO27000 series of standards, and to compare them with the adoption rates for ISO9000 and ISO14000. The paper aims to compare the barriers to adoption for the different standards. Previous studies suggest that ISO27001 adoption is slower than for the other standards. The uptake of ISO27001 has been slower than the related management system standards ISO9001 and ISO14001, with approximately half the certifications compared with ISO14001. In response to the issues raised in this analysis, the paper considers how an approach based on a maturity model can be used to help overcome these barriers, especially in smaller companies. The 2008 survey of ISO27001-certificated companies found that 50 per cent of the certificated organisations which responded had fewer than 200 employees, and were therefore in the SME category. Perhaps more surprisingly, around half of these had fewer than 50 employees The framework has used the ISO27002 code of practice to define the elements, which should be considered within the ISMS. Each element is then developed through a maturity model lifecycle to develop processes to the point where an ISO27001-compliant ISMS can be implemented. The principal contribution of the paper is a step-by-step framework designed to simplify the process for organisations working towards ISO27001 and offer significant benefits at milestones before systems are mature enough to achieve certification. © 2011, Emerald Group Publishing Limited","methods":"Comparative conceptual paper reviewing global adoption of ISO27000, ISO9000, and ISO14000 and their adoption barriers. It proposes a maturity-model framework based on ISO27002 to guide ISMS development toward ISO27001 compliance.","results":"ISO27001 adoption is slower than ISO9001 and ISO14001, with approximately half the certifications of ISO14001. In the 2008 survey, 50% of responding certificated organisations had fewer than 200 employees and about half of those had fewer than 50 employees.","conclusions":"A step-by-step maturity-model framework can simplify ISO27001 adoption and provide benefits before certification. The approach is especially intended to help smaller companies overcome adoption barriers.","publisher":"Emerald Publishing","open":false,"insight":"Un modelo de madurez por pasos facilita la adopción en pymes y aporta valor antes de llegar a la certificación.","tag":"Pymes"},{"n":9,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.2307/jj.12525375","authors":"Kenyon, B.","year":"2024","journal":"ISO 27001 Controls: A Guide to Implementing and Auditing, Second Edition","title":"ISO 27001 Controls: A guide to implementing and auditing, Second edition","abstract":"Following the success of the first edition, this book has been re-released to reflect the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and ISO/IEC 27002:2022 updates. Ideal for information security managers, auditors, consultants and organisations preparing for ISO 27001:2022 certification, this book will help readers understand the requirements of an ISMS (information security management system) based on ISO 27001:2022. Similarly, for anyone involved in internal or external audits, the book includes the definitive requirements that auditors must address when certifying organisations to ISO 27001:2022. The auditing guidance covers what evidence an auditor should look for to satisfy themselves that the requirement has been met. This guidance is useful for internal auditors and consultants, as well as information security managers and lead implementers as a means of confirming that their implementation and evidence to support it will be sufficient to pass an audit. This guide is intended to be used by those involved in: Designing, implementing and/or maintaining an ISMS; Preparing for ISMS audits and assessments; or Undertaking both internal and third-party ISMS audits and assessments.","methods":"Second edition guide/book aligned to ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and ISO/IEC 27002:2022, focused on implementing, maintaining, and auditing an ISMS. No formal research method or search approach is described.","results":"Provides implementation and auditing guidance for ISO 27001:2022 certification, including the evidence auditors should look for to verify requirements are met. Intended for information security managers, auditors, consultants, and lead implementers.","conclusions":"The book is presented as a practical guide to help organizations prepare for and pass ISO 27001:2022 audits and certification. It does not report empirical findings and offers no explicit limitations in the abstract.","publisher":"De Gruyter","open":false,"insight":"La auditoría exige evidencia verificable de cada requisito y control, no declaraciones de intención ni documentos sin aplicar.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":10,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-84078-4_29","authors":"Ruete Zuñiga, D.R.; Lira, P.L.; Navarro, A.C.; ... Maidana, J.P.M.","year":"2025","journal":"Communications in Computer and Information Science","title":"Agile Audit Proposal for ISO IEC 27001 Standard Implementation in Higher Education Faculties","abstract":"The Information Security Management System (ISMS) is responsible for protecting information by implementing strategies to ensure its confidentiality, availability, and integrity. Information for the ISMS is collected through a Statement of Applicability, allowing for an ISMS audit of the organization and demonstrating its condition. This work proposes an agile internal audit methodology based on ISO/IEC 27001, aimed at reducing the time required for internal audits and preparing the institution for formal certification.","methods":"Proposes an agile internal audit methodology based on ISO/IEC 27001 for higher education faculties; no empirical sample or formal analysis is described in the abstract.","results":"No empirical results are reported; the proposal is intended to reduce internal audit time and prepare the institution for formal certification.","conclusions":"The authors conclude that an agile ISO/IEC 27001 internal audit approach may streamline audits and support certification readiness.","publisher":"Springer Nature","open":false,"insight":"Auditorías internas ágiles y frecuentes reducen el tiempo de revisión y preparan mejor la certificación.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":11,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app12094102","authors":"Antunes, M.; Maximiano, M.; Gomes, R.","year":"2022","journal":"Applied Sciences (Switzerland)","title":"A Client-Centered Information Security and Cybersecurity Auditing Framework","abstract":"Information security and cybersecurity management play a key role in modern enterprises. There is a plethora of standards, frameworks, and tools, ISO 27000 and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework being two relevant families of international Information Security Management Standards (ISMSs). Globally, these standards are implemented by dedicated tools to collect and further analyze the information security auditing that is carried out in an enterprise. The overall goal of the auditing is to evaluate and mitigate the information security risk. The risk assessment is grounded by auditing processes, which examine and assess a list of predefined controls in a wide variety of subjects regarding cybersecurity and information security. For each control, a checklist of actions is applied and a set of corrective measures is proposed, in order to mitigate the flaws and to increase the level of compliance with the standard being used. The auditing process can apply different ISMSs in the same time frame. However, as these processes are time-consuming, involve on-site interventions, and imply specialized consulting teams, the methodology usually adopted by enterprises consists of applying a single ISMS and its existing tools and frameworks. This strategy brings overall less flexibility and diversity to the auditing process and, consequently, to the assessment results of the audited enterprise. In a broad sense, the auditing needs of Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs) are different from large companies and do not fit with all the existing ISMSs’ frameworks, that is a set of controls of a particular ISMS is not suitable to be applied in an auditing process, in an SME. In this paper, we propose a generic and client-centered web-integrated cybersecurity auditing information system. The proposed system can be widely used in a myriad of auditing processes, as it is flexible and it can load a set of predefined controls’ checklist assessment and their corresponding mitigation tasks’ list. It was designed to meet both SMEs’ and large enterprises’ requirements and stores auditing and intervention-related data in a relational database. The information system was tested within an ISO 27001:2013 information security auditing project, in which fifty SMEs participated. The overall architecture and design are depicted and the global results are detailed in this paper.","methods":"Proposed a generic client-centered web-integrated cybersecurity auditing information system, tested in an ISO 27001:2013 auditing project with 50 SMEs. The system stores auditing and intervention data in a relational database.","results":"The system was tested in an ISO 27001:2013 information security auditing project involving 50 SMEs. The paper reports the overall architecture, design, and global results, but no specific performance metrics are given in the abstract.","conclusions":"The framework is intended to be flexible and usable across SMEs and large enterprises, improving auditing adaptability beyond single-ISMS approaches. The abstract does not state any limitations.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"Un marco de auditoría flexible y centrado en el cliente se adapta tanto a pymes como a grandes organizaciones.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":12,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICECCE63537.2024.10823548","authors":"Salihu, A.; Dervishi, R.","year":"2024","journal":"5th International Conference on Electrical, Communication and Computer Engineering, ICECCE 2024","title":"Evaluating the Impact of Risk Management Frameworks on IT Audits: A Comparative Analysis of COSO, COBIT, ISO/IEC 27001, and NIST CSF","abstract":"In today's digital landscape, information technology (IT) audits are critical for ensuring an in digital landscape nowadays. Frameworks related to risk management provide a systematic approach to identify and reduce IT risks. Control frameworks provide a defined method for analyzing internal controls and ensuring the effectiveness of IT audits. This study focuses on the impact and effectiveness of four major frameworks on IT audits: COSO, COBIT, ISO/IEC 27001, and NIST CSF. We investigate how each framework organizes the audit process, aids in control gap discovery, improves audit efficiency, and allows for unambiguous reporting. In addition, we explain factors to consider while picking the best framework for an organization's specific risk profile. The study conducts a thorough assessment of their strengths, shortcomings, and applicability for various IT audit scenarios.","methods":"Comparative analysis of four risk management frameworks—COSO, COBIT, ISO/IEC 27001, and NIST CSF—focused on IT audits. The abstract describes a thorough assessment of strengths, shortcomings, and applicability, but no formal study sample or analytical method is given.","results":"The frameworks are presented as helping organize the audit process, discover control gaps, improve audit efficiency, and support unambiguous reporting. The paper also compares their strengths, shortcomings, and suitability across IT audit scenarios.","conclusions":"Different frameworks are better suited to different organizational risk profiles and IT audit scenarios. No explicit limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"Cada marco encaja con perfiles de riesgo distintos; la elección debe responder al contexto y al tipo de auditoría.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":13,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/AQTR70159.2026.11577668","authors":"Priescu, C.-M.; Moisescu, M.-A.; Pietraru, R.N.","year":"2026","journal":"IEEE International Conference on Automation, Quality and Testing, Robotics, AQTR","title":"Automated Information Security Policy Analysis: A Conceptual Framework","abstract":"Manual policy review is a demanding and laborious process that auditors routinely engage in as part of compliance audits. This paper proposes a framework for policy analysis automation as part of an information security audit, thereby enhancing auditors' overall efficiency and streamlining the evidence review process. Specifically, this research introduces a conceptual framework employing Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques to automate the analysis of information security policies against ISO/IEC 27001:2022 documentation provisions. To ensure full automation, the policy is evaluated by distinct requirement sets that check: document structural validation, standard-derived semantic evaluations and common security practice adoption. Additionally, text quality is included in the assessment by defining elements that account for global policy coherence and contradiction detection. Moreover, the framework proposes two text quality metrics to facilitate standardized benchmarking and a general compliance score. This study contributes to compliance automation by addressing the assessment of information security policies, as a key audit artifact.","methods":"Conceptual framework using NLP to automate analysis of information security policies against ISO/IEC 27001:2022 provisions, including structural validation, semantic evaluation, security practice checks, and text-quality assessment.","results":"Proposes two text-quality metrics and a general compliance score for standardized benchmarking of policy analysis.","conclusions":"The framework is intended to improve auditors' efficiency and streamline evidence review in compliance audits; no empirical validation results are reported in the abstract.","publisher":"IEEE","open":true,"insight":"Automatizar el análisis de políticas acelera la revisión de evidencias, sin sustituir el juicio del auditor.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":14,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.58482/ijeresm.v4i3.2","authors":"Hanif, M.F.; Harahap, A.R.; Fakhrudin, A.; Febriawan, D.","year":"2025","journal":"International Journal of Emerging Research in Engineering, Science, and Management","title":"Integrating COBIT and ISO Frameworks in IT Audits: A Literature Review","abstract":"The accelerated evolution of information technology (IT) has compelled organizations to adopt structured governance frameworks to enhance audit efficacy and ensure robust information security. This study presents a systematic literature review examining the integration of COBIT and ISO/IEC 27001 within IT audit practices. Employing a qualitative descriptive methodology, the review synthesizes insights from seven primary scholarly sources, including case studies from both public and private sectors. The analysis delineates integration patterns, identifies best practices, and explores the synergistic potential of aligning COBIT's strategic governance capabilities with the technical control rigour of ISO/IEC 27001. Findings demonstrate that such integration enhances audit capability maturity, facilitates structured risk mitigation, and fosters alignment between IT functions and organizational objectives. Nonetheless, notable research gaps persist, particularly the scarcity of quantitative assessments, limited cross-sector generalizability, and the absence of longitudinal evaluations of implementation outcomes. Additionally, practical challenges—including integration complexity, inadequate human resource competencies, and the lack of standardized implementation guidelines—impede broader adoption. The study concludes that integrating COBIT and ISO/IEC 27001 constitutes a viable foundation for advancing IT governance and audit maturity. However, further empirical investigation and development of pragmatic toolkits are essential. These insights aim to inform auditors, IT governance professionals, and policy makers in devising adaptive, standard-aligned audit strategies.","methods":"Systematic literature review using a qualitative descriptive methodology. Synthesizes seven primary scholarly sources, including public- and private-sector case studies.","results":"Integration of COBIT and ISO/IEC 27001 improved audit capability maturity, structured risk mitigation, and alignment between IT functions and organizational objectives. Integration patterns, best practices, and synergies were identified.","conclusions":"Integrating COBIT and ISO/IEC 27001 is a viable foundation for advancing IT governance and audit maturity. Key limitations were scarce quantitative assessments, limited cross-sector generalizability, and no longitudinal outcome evaluations.","publisher":"JPM Publishers","open":true,"insight":"Integrar COBIT e ISO 27001 madura la auditoría y alinea TI con el negocio; aún faltan evaluaciones cuantitativas.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":15,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/Confluence60223.2024.10463392","authors":"Chavez, S.; Anahue, J.; Ticona, W.","year":"2024","journal":"Proceedings of the 14th International Conference on Cloud Computing, Data Science and Engineering, Confluence 2024","title":"Implementation of an ISMS Based on ISO/IEC 27001:2022 to Improve Information Security in the Internet Services Sector","abstract":"Information security is currently crucial for all organizations, and the implementation of an Information Security Management System (ISMS) based on ISO/IEC 27001:2022 is presented as a framework of excellence to address threats. cyber and protect information assets. This research will use the MAGERIT methodology to analyze and manage information systems, integrating it with the ISMS to identify assets, analyze risks, determine safeguards, and establish risk treatment measures. As a result of the implementation, there is an increase in confidentiality controls from 9 to 12, and a reduction in incidents from 40 to 12. Likewise, there is an increase in integrity controls from 9 to 15, and a reduction in incidents from 52 to 30. Regarding to availability, there is an increase in controls from 9 to 18 and a decrease in incidents from 49 to 29; thus, demonstrating a successful implementation in the evaluated categories. Finally, it is important to highlight information security in the internet services sector to protect data, mitigate risks, ensure customer trust, and comply with legal regulations in an increasingly threatening digital environment.","methods":"ISMS implementation based on ISO/IEC 27001:2022 using the MAGERIT methodology to identify assets, analyze risks, determine safeguards, and define risk treatment measures.","results":"Confidentiality controls increased from 9 to 12 and incidents fell from 40 to 12; integrity controls increased from 9 to 15 and incidents fell from 52 to 30; availability controls increased from 9 to 18 and incidents fell from 49 to 29.","conclusions":"The implementation was successful in the evaluated categories and is presented as important for protecting data, mitigating risks, ensuring customer trust, and complying with legal regulations. No limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"Un SGSI 27001:2022 bien implantado protege datos, mitiga riesgos y sostiene la confianza del cliente.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":16,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/INFINITYS69204.2026.11666096","authors":"Novandra, Y.; Aqila, K.S.; Kilmakossu, G.V.C.; ... Legowo, N.","year":"2026","journal":"Proceedings of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Instrumentation, Networks, Future Sustainability, Information Theory, Transportation, and Systems, INFINITYS 2026","title":"ISO/IEC 27001:2022-based Risk Management Framework in Sharia Insurance Company","abstract":"The increasing reliance on information systems in regulated financial institutions highlights the importance of structured information security risk management. This study examines the implementation of an ISO/IEC 27001:2022 based risk management framework in a sharia life insurance company in Indonesia. Using a qualitative descriptive approach, the study identifies key cyber threats and evaluates information security risks based on likelihood, impact, and confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA) principles. The results show that risks related to core system availability and data privacy compliance represent the highest priorities due to their impact on business continuity, regulatory obligations, and the Sharia principle of amanah. Medium-level risks, including phishing attacks and unauthorized data modification, require strengthened controls and continuous monitoring, while low-level risks may be accepted with routine oversight. This study demonstrates that a risk-based ISMS aligned with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 effectively supports risk prioritization, regulatory compliance, and continuous improvement in sharia insurance organizations.","methods":"Qualitative descriptive study of ISO/IEC 27001:2022-based risk management in a sharia life insurance company in Indonesia, assessing cyber threats and information security risks using likelihood, impact, and CIA principles.","results":"Core system availability and data privacy compliance were the highest-priority risks due to business continuity, regulatory obligations, and the Sharia principle of amanah. Phishing and unauthorized data modification were medium risks; low-level risks were acceptable with routine oversight.","conclusions":"A risk-based ISMS aligned with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 supports risk prioritization, regulatory compliance, and continuous improvement in sharia insurance organizations.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":true,"insight":"El enfoque basado en riesgo prioriza controles y facilita el cumplimiento regulatorio en el sector asegurador.","tag":"Riesgo"},{"n":17,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1757-899X/852/1/012128","authors":"Gunawan, N.K.; Hadiprakoso, R.B.; Kabetta, H.","year":"2020","journal":"IOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering","title":"Comparative study between the integration of ITIL and ISO / IEC 27001 with the integration of COBIT and ISO / IEC 27001","abstract":"This paper tries to get a comparison between the integration of Information Technology Infrastructure Library (ITIL) and ISO / IEC 27001 with Integration of COBIT and ISO / IEC 27001. Factors to be considered are the increased level of information security services credibility, cost of the two standards integration of, the time of implementation, the multiple processes removal, and the increase in understanding between parts of the organization. A literature study is used as a research method. The results of the research can be a reference for organizations to consider two information security standards that will be integrated.","methods":"Literature study comparing integration of ITIL + ISO/IEC 27001 versus COBIT + ISO/IEC 27001, focusing on security service credibility, cost, implementation time, process removal, and organizational understanding.","results":"The study provides a comparison of the two integration options across credibility, cost, implementation time, process removal, and organizational understanding.","conclusions":"The results can serve as a reference for organizations choosing between the two integrated information security standards.","publisher":"Institute of Physics Publishing","open":true,"insight":"Integrar ISO con ITIL o COBIT es viable; la elección depende de si se busca operación de servicios o gobierno.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":18,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1757-899X/879/1/012047","authors":"Prapenan, G.G.; Pamuji, G.C.","year":"2020","journal":"IOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering","title":"Information System Security Analysis of XYZ Company Using COBIT 5 Framework and ISO 27001:2013","abstract":"Information system security is now very important to prevent from an attack. Besides that, low level security can harm the productivity of an organization. The purpose of this research is to ascertain and know the level of information system security in XYZ Company, because it has never conducted an audit information system security. In this research, it used the COBIT 5 framework to audit information security management system in a company based on the ISO 27001 standard. This audit was conducted to identify information system security that was not good. In addition to improving the information security management system that will be adjusted to the ISO 27001 standard. This research was composed by applying a qualitative methodology, observation of the activity in the company and reviews related information security management system literature document existing. For the auditing using COBIT 5 framework, audit focuses on four kinds process that consists of APO12, DSS05, MEA02 dan EDM03. The audit COBIT 5 two results in consisting of \"Managed Process\"Level on APO12, MEA02 and EDM03 and \"Performed Process\"level on DSS05. The resulting of this research will be used a reference to the improvement of existing information system security in the company.","methods":"Qualitative audit of XYZ Company’s information security using the COBIT 5 framework aligned with ISO 27001:2013, based on observation and literature/document review. The audit focused on APO12, DSS05, MEA02, and EDM03.","results":"APO12, MEA02, and EDM03 were at the Managed Process level, while DSS05 was at the Performed Process level.","conclusions":"The audit identified areas where information security was not good and the findings were intended to guide improvements to the company’s information security management system in line with ISO 27001:2013.","publisher":"Institute of Physics Publishing","open":true,"insight":"La auditoría combinada revela brechas que una revisión técnica aislada no detecta.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":19,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSPIS59665.2023.10402713","authors":"Hadad, R.S.A.; Maulana, H.","year":"2023","journal":"ICSPIS 2023 - Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Signal Processing and Intelligent Systems","title":"A Comprehensive Review of COBIT and ISO 27001: Approaches to Auditing Credit Bureau Automation System (CBAS) at PT XYZ","abstract":"This study evaluates, compares and discuss the approaches of COBIT and ISO 27001 in auditing the Credit Bureau Automation System (CBAS) at PT XYZ. The authors employ a qualitative comparative analysis to explore and synthesize qualitative information from scholarly sources. This study concludes that ISO 27001 is better suited for auditing CBAS as it has a strong focus on information security and risk management, as well as flexibility to tailor to client needs. In contrast, COBIT has a more generalized scope and covers broader IT topics. However, the authors suggest that both frameworks can complement each other in an audit, depending on the organization's specific needs. This study also highlights the strengths of COBIT, such as its comprehensive framework that enables organizations to attain their goals in corporate IT governance and management, and suggests a simplified IT risk management maturity audit system based on 'COBIT 5 for Risk' to evaluate the maturity of IT risk management in an organization. Overall, this study provides valuable insights into the applicability of COBIT and ISO 27001 in auditing CBAS and offers suggestions for organizations to improve their IT governance and risk management practices.","methods":"Qualitative comparative analysis of COBIT and ISO 27001 using scholarly sources to audit CBAS at PT XYZ.","results":"ISO 27001 was found better suited for auditing CBAS because of its focus on information security, risk management, and flexibility; COBIT was broader and more generalized.","conclusions":"ISO 27001 is recommended for CBAS auditing, while COBIT and ISO 27001 can complement each other depending on organizational needs. A simplified IT risk management maturity audit system based on COBIT 5 for Risk is suggested.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"ISO 27001 y COBIT se complementan: ISO aporta controles y COBIT madurez de gobierno y gestión del riesgo.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":20,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/standards5040033","authors":"Metin, B.; Sevim, S.B.; Wynn, M.","year":"2025","journal":"Standards","title":"Cybersecurity Strategy Development: Towards an Integrated Approach Based on COBIT and ISO 27000 Series Standards","abstract":"This article presents a practical guide for developing a cybersecurity strategy that integrates COBIT 2019 with the ISO/IEC 27000 series of standards. Although COBIT 2019 provides strong frameworks for IT strategy and governance, it does not specifically prescribe a cybersecurity strategy. This article addresses this gap in the strategy literature by building upon the ISO/IEC 27000 series, which is designed to be adaptable for organizations of all types and sizes, as well as being suitable for various regulatory and technological environments. First, a synthesis of COBIT 2019 and the ISO/IEC standards (particularly 27014, 27001, 27036, and 27701) identifies six key themes for a cybersecurity strategy. A more specific qualitative content analysis of ISO/IEC 27014 (which focuses on board-level information security governance) and COBIT 2019 (which outlines execution mechanics) confirms the validity of these themes with traceability at the clause and objective levels. To operationalize these themes, a three-step method is put forward: setting alignment objectives and scope; translating these into IT strategy decisions using COBIT governance and management objectives and practices; and establishing a cybersecurity strategy through ISO/IEC 27001. Additionally, ISO/IEC 27701 for privacy and ISO/IEC 27036 for supplier governance are incorporated where relevant. An illustrative example is provided using anonymized data from public sources, and the applicability and limitations of the research findings are discussed.","methods":"Practical guide integrating COBIT 2019 with the ISO/IEC 27000 series, using synthesis and qualitative content analysis of ISO/IEC 27014 and COBIT 2019. A three-step method is proposed and illustrated with anonymized public-source data.","results":"Synthesis identified six key themes for a cybersecurity strategy, and qualitative content analysis confirmed them with clause- and objective-level traceability. The paper also proposes a three-step operational method and incorporates ISO/IEC 27701 and 27036 where relevant.","conclusions":"The integrated COBIT- and ISO-based approach offers a practical way to develop cybersecurity strategy where COBIT alone is insufficient. The authors note that applicability and limitations are discussed, but no specific limitation is detailed in the abstract.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"COBIT por sí solo no basta para una estrategia de ciberseguridad; la integración con la familia ISO 27000 aporta controles.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":21,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSSA51342.2020.9310875","authors":"Yasin, M.; Arman, A.; Edward, I.J.M.; Shalannanda, W.","year":"2020","journal":"Proceeding of 14th International Conference on Telecommunication Systems, Services, and Applications, TSSA 2020","title":"Designing information security governance recommendations and roadmap using COBIT 2019 Framework and ISO 27001:2013 (Case Study Ditreskrimsus Polda XYZ)","abstract":"The use of technology has applied in all areas of Polri's duties. However, the use of this technology does not yet have a level of capability in information security management. For this reason, it is necessary to design recommendations and an ideal information governance roadmap based on COBIT 2019 and ISO/IEC 27001: 2013 concerning Information Security Management Systems (ISMS). The design is carried out based on six stages in the Design Science Research Methodology (DSRM) in the form of identify problems and motivate, define objects of a solution, design and development, demonstration, evaluation, and communication. By mapping ISO/IEC 27001: 2013 into COBIT 2019, 29 domains of the 2019 COBIT core model selected which became the basis for designing and assessing the level of information security management capability at Ditreskrimsus Polda XYZ. The formulation of recommendations considered the assessment results. It produced the model of organizational structure, human resources, and policies and procedures that must be applied to Ditreskrimsus Polda XYZ in the form of a roadmap starting in 2021-2025 in managing information security. This research contributes to producing an information security governance design.","methods":"Design Science Research Methodology (DSRM) with six stages: problem identification, solution definition, design/development, demonstration, evaluation, and communication. ISO/IEC 27001:2013 was mapped to COBIT 2019 core model domains to assess information security management capability at Ditreskrimsus Polda XYZ.","results":"Mapping yielded 29 COBIT 2019 core model domains as the basis for capability assessment and recommendation design. The output was a 2021-2025 roadmap covering organizational structure, human resources, and policies and procedures for information security management.","conclusions":"The study produced an information security governance design and roadmap for Ditreskrimsus Polda XYZ. It is limited to a single case study context.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"Un roadmap de gobierno de seguridad requiere diagnóstico, priorización y responsables; un solo caso limita generalizar.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":22,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.4018/978-1-6684-3698-1.ch001","authors":"Sabillón, R.","year":"2021","journal":"Research Anthology on Business Aspects of Cybersecurity","title":"Audits in Cybersecurity","abstract":"The objective of this chapter is to provision a comprehensive literature review of the most relevant approaches for conducting cybersecurity audits. The study includes auditing perspectives for specific scopes and the best practices that many leading organizations are providing for security and auditing professionals to follow. The chapter reviews relevant features for auditing approaches in the following order: ISO/IEC 27001:2013, ISO/IEC 27002:2013, Control Objectives for Information and Related Technology (COBIT) 2019, Information Technology Infrastructure Library (ITIL) 4, AICPA, ISACA, NIST SP 800-53, NIST CSF v1.1, IIA, PCI DSS, ITAF, COSO, ENISA, NERC CIP, and CSAM.","methods":"Narrative literature review chapter on cybersecurity audits, organizing relevant approaches and best practices across ISO/IEC 27001/27002, COBIT 2019, ITIL 4, AICPA, ISACA, NIST SP 800-53, NIST CSF v1.1, IIA, PCI DSS, ITAF, COSO, ENISA, NERC CIP, and CSAM.","results":"The chapter surveys auditing perspectives for specific scopes and summarizes best practices from leading organizations for security and auditing professionals.","conclusions":"Cybersecurity audit guidance is best understood through multiple standards and frameworks rather than a single approach.","publisher":"IGI Global","open":false,"insight":"La auditoría de ciberseguridad se entiende mejor combinando varios estándares y marcos, no uno solo.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":23,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICISS71466.2026.11715036","authors":"Cordelia, E.; Nugraha, I.G.B.B.","year":"2026","journal":"2026 13th International Conference on ICT for Smart Society, ICISS 2026","title":"Designing the Governance of a Container Inspection Automation System at the Port","abstract":"Port container inspection is still largely manual and fragmented, with isolated records, no centralized audit trail, and no service level instrument on the terminal-operator side. Introducing artificial intelligence (AI) into this process raises the need for auditable governance rather than only technical capability. This study designs an information technology (IT) governance framework for Cargovision, an AI-based container inspection system, aligned with COBIT 2019, ITIL 4, and International Organization for Standardization/International Electrotechnical Commission (ISO/IEC) 27001. Because no pairwise comparison matrix or consistency ratio was available, the model is selected through empirical multi-criteria weighted scoring informed by organizational priorities and practitioner judgement; the integrated COBIT-ITIL model scored 0.93. The framework specifies a Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, and Informed (RACI) matrix, four service level parameters, data and audit-trail policy, an anti-corruption layer, explainability and human-in-the-loop controls, and an authority boundary across entities. It is evaluated in a controlled test environment against eighteen confidential internal documents and through interviews with three terminal practitioners. The result is a proof of concept that directs IT governance maturity from Level 2 toward Level 3; operational validation remains required.","methods":"Design study of an IT governance framework for Cargovision, aligned with COBIT 2019, ITIL 4, and ISO/IEC 27001. The model was selected using empirical multi-criteria weighted scoring informed by organizational priorities and practitioner judgement, and evaluated in a controlled test environment with 18 internal documents and interviews with 3 terminal practitioners.","results":"The integrated COBIT-ITIL model scored 0.93. The framework defined a RACI matrix, four service level parameters, data and audit-trail policy, anti-corruption layer, explainability and human-in-the-loop controls, and an authority boundary across entities; it directed governance maturity from Level 2 toward Level 3.","conclusions":"The study produced a proof of concept for auditable AI governance in port container inspection. Operational validation is still required.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"La gobernanza auditable de sistemas automatizados exige validación operativa, no solo un buen diseño.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":24,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.17013/risti.22.73-88","authors":"Valencia Duque, F.J.; Orozco-Alzate, M.","year":"2017","journal":"RISTI - Revista Iberica de Sistemas e Tecnologias de Informacao","title":"A methodology for implementing an information security management system based on the family of ISO/IEC 27000 standards","abstract":"A methodology for the implementation of an Information Security Management System (ISMS) based on the ISO/IEC 27000 family of standards is proposed, with an emphasis on the interrelationship of four fundamental standards which break down the activities to be developed in order to comply with the requirements established in the ISO/IEC 27001, the safety controls presented in the ISO/IEC 27002, the ISO/IEC 27005 risk scheme and the steps recommended in the ISO/IEC 27003. The result is a methodological process that explains how to face a project of this level of importance in the current context of organizations and based on international standards. This methodological process represents a contribution to the professionals who undertake this work, and who are looking for a method to carry out a successful implementation of an ISMS.","methods":"Proposes a methodology for implementing an ISMS based on the ISO/IEC 27000 family, emphasizing the interrelationship of ISO/IEC 27001, 27002, 27005, and 27003.","results":"The result is a methodological process that explains how to manage an ISMS implementation project based on international standards.","conclusions":"The methodology is presented as a contribution for professionals seeking a successful ISMS implementation. No specific limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Associacao Iberica de Sistemas e Tecnologias de Informacao","open":true,"insight":"Una metodología basada en la familia ISO 27000 guía una implantación ordenada del SGSI.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":25,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1108/TQM-09-2020-0202","authors":"Culot, G.; Nassimbeni, G.; Podrecca, M.; Sartor, M.","year":"2021","journal":"TQM Journal","title":"The ISO/IEC 27001 information security management standard: literature review and theory-based research agenda","abstract":"Purpose: After 15 years of research, this paper aims to present a review of the academic literature on the ISO/IEC 27001, the most renowned standard for information security and the third most widespread ISO certification. Emerging issues are reframed through the lenses of social systems thinking, deriving a theory-based research agenda to inspire interdisciplinary studies in the field. Design/methodology/approach: The study is structured as a systematic literature review. Findings: Research themes and sub-themes are identified on five broad research foci: relation with other standards, motivations, issues in the implementation, possible outcomes and contextual factors. Originality/value: The study presents a structured overview of the academic body of knowledge on ISO/IEC 27001, providing solid foundations for future research on the topic. A set of research opportunities is outlined, with the aim to inspire future interdisciplinary studies at the crossroad between information security and quality management. Managers interested in the implementation of the standard and policymakers can find an overview of academic knowledge useful to inform their decisions related to implementation and regulatory activities.","methods":"Systematic literature review of academic research on ISO/IEC 27001, reframed through social systems thinking to derive a theory-based research agenda.","results":"Identified research themes and sub-themes across five foci: relation with other standards, motivations, implementation issues, possible outcomes, and contextual factors.","conclusions":"Provides a structured overview of ISO/IEC 27001 literature and outlines research opportunities for future interdisciplinary studies; useful for managers and policymakers. No key empirical limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Emerald Publishing","open":true,"insight":"La investigación sobre ISO 27001 reclama estudios interdisciplinares y evidencia empírica más sólida.","tag":"Evidencia"},{"n":26,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp1020012","authors":"Antunes, M.; Maximiano, M.; Gomes, R.; Pinto, D.","year":"2021","journal":"Journal of Cybersecurity and Privacy","title":"Information Security and Cybersecurity Management: A Case Study with SMEs in Portugal","abstract":"Information security plays a key role in enterprises management, as it deals with the confidentiality, privacy, integrity, and availability of one of their most valuable resources: data and information. Small and Medium-sized enterprises (SME) are seen as a blind spot in information security and cybersecurity management, which is mainly due to their size, regional and familiar scope, and financial resources. This paper presents an information security and cybersecurity management project, in which a methodology based on the well-known ISO-27001:2013 standard was designed and implemented in fifty SMEs that were located in the center region of Portugal. The project was conducted by a business association located at the center of Portugal and mainly participated by SMEs. The Polytechnic of Leiria and an IT auditing/consulting team were the other two entities that participated on the project. The characterisation of the participating enterprises, the ISO-27001:2013 based methodology developed and implemented in SMEs, as well as the results obtained in this case study, are depicted and analysed in the paper. The attained results show a clear benefit to the audited and intervened SMEs, being mainly attested by the increasing of their information security management robustness and collaborators’ cyberawareness.","methods":"Case study of an information security and cybersecurity management project using an ISO-27001:2013-based methodology implemented in 50 SMEs in central Portugal.","results":"The intervention showed clear benefits for the audited SMEs, mainly increased information security management robustness and collaborators’ cyberawareness.","conclusions":"The study concludes that the ISO-27001:2013-based approach benefited SMEs; limitations are not stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"El enfoque ISO 27001 aporta beneficios a pymes, pero requiere adaptación a su madurez y recursos.","tag":"Pymes"},{"n":27,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TEM.2020.2977815","authors":"Mirtsch, M.; Kinne, J.; Blind, K.","year":"2021","journal":"IEEE Transactions on Engineering Management","title":"Exploring the Adoption of the International Information Security Management System Standard ISO/IEC 27001: A Web Mining-Based Analysis","abstract":"In the light of digitalization and recent EU policy initiatives, information is an important asset that organizations of all sizes and from all sectors should secure. However, in order to provide common requirements for the implementation of an information security management system, the internationally well-accepted ISO/IEC 27001 standard has not shown the expected growth rate since its publication more than a decade ago. In this article, we apply web mining to explore the adoption of ISO/IEC 27001 through a series of 2664 out of more than 900 000 German firms from the Mannheim Enterprise Panel dataset that refers to this standard on their websites. As a result, we present a ''landscape'' of ISO/IEC 27001 in Germany, which shows that firms not only seek certifications themselves but often refer on their websites to partners who are certified instead. Consequently, we estimate a probit model and find that larger and more innovative firms are more likely to be certified to ISO/IEC 27001 and that almost half of all certified firms belong to the information and communications technology (ICT) service sector. Based on our findings, we derive implications for policy makers and management and critically assess the suitability of web mining to explore the adoption of management system standards.","methods":"Web mining of 2,664 German firms from the Mannheim Enterprise Panel dataset; a probit model was estimated to analyze ISO/IEC 27001 adoption.","results":"Larger and more innovative firms were more likely to be certified to ISO/IEC 27001, and almost half of certified firms were in the ICT service sector. Firms often referred on their websites to certified partners rather than being certified themselves.","conclusions":"The study derives implications for policy makers and management and concludes that web mining is suitable for exploring management system standard adoption, though it is critically assessed.","publisher":"IEEE","open":true,"insight":"La difusión de ISO 27001 puede estudiarse a gran escala; la adopción depende de regulación, sector y comercio.","tag":"Evidencia"},{"n":28,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10799-018-00297-3","authors":"Deane, J.K.; Goldberg, D.M.; Rakes, T.R.; Rees, L.P.","year":"2019","journal":"Information Technology and Management","title":"The effect of information security certification announcements on the market value of the firm","abstract":"Information security management has increasingly been recognized as one of the major business challenges of the last decade. While security research has widely recognized that breaches are detrimental to business value, the other side of the equation has received little attention. The literature on the value impact of proactive financial investments into information security management infrastructure and policy is very limited. Unlike most information technology investments, reinforcements to information security management programs suggest a reduction of a firm’s risk of damages in future attacks rather than an improvement in a firm’s revenue generation. Furthermore, contemporary information security management represents a process-based shift in a firm’s operations. In light of the unique information security risks faced by modern firms, we posit several hypotheses related to the value created from information security management program investments. We then present an empirical examination of the effects of information security management program investments on shareholder value. We use a firm’s successful completion of the ISO 27001 certification requirements as evidence of its commitment to developing a robust information security management program. Based on 111 public announcements, we find that the associated abnormal stock market reaction is both positive and statistically significant. We further control for firms’ industries, sizes, and dates of certification, and we find that they all affect the mean abnormal returns observed. This study demonstrates the capacity for information security management program investments to generate value for firms and further offers guidance for practitioners seeking to maximize shareholder value.","methods":"Empirical examination of shareholder value effects using 111 public ISO 27001 certification announcements as evidence of information security management program investment.","results":"The abnormal stock market reaction to the announcements was positive and statistically significant. Firm industry, size, and certification date also affected mean abnormal returns.","conclusions":"Information security management program investments can create firm value and provide guidance for practitioners seeking to maximize shareholder value.","publisher":"Springer Nature","open":false,"insight":"Anunciar la certificación puede crear valor de mercado y confianza en los inversores.","tag":"Valor"},{"n":29,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/HICSS.2016.600","authors":"Hsu, C.; Wang, T.; Lu, A.","year":"2016","journal":"Proceedings of the Annual Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences","title":"The impact of ISO 27001 certification on firm performance","abstract":"The extensive organizational dependence on information technology (IT), along with worsening impact of information security incidents, has made information security one of the top management concerns. The ISO 27001 standard provides guidance to a sound information security management system (ISMS). However, implementation and accreditation costs can also be considerable. In this study, we explored whether the certification can benefit organizations by signaling the management's attitude toward security management and the appropriateness of ISMS implementation. We investigated firm performance after the ISO 27001 certification with samples from the United States and selected European countries. Different from our expectation, we found no evidence that ISO 27001 certification brought benefits to the certified firm in terms of return-on-assets and stock market performance. We attributed the results to the nature of ISO 27001 that a good information security management would be seen as an obligation, instead of a competitive advantage.","methods":"Comparative study of firm performance after ISO 27001 certification using samples from the United States and selected European countries.","results":"No evidence that ISO 27001 certification improved return on assets or stock market performance.","conclusions":"ISO 27001 certification did not appear to benefit firm performance; the authors suggest security management is viewed as an obligation rather than a competitive advantage.","publisher":"IEEE","open":false,"insight":"Certificar por obligación no garantiza mejor desempeño; la seguridad debe verse como ventaja, no solo como sello.","tag":"Valor"},{"n":30,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ARES.2014.41","authors":"Shojaie, B.; Federrath, H.; Saberi, I.","year":"2014","journal":"Proceedings - 9th International Conference on Availability, Reliability and Security, ARES 2014","title":"Evaluating the effectiveness of ISO 27001: 2013 based on annex A","abstract":"The part of the management system of an organization dealing with information security is called Information Security Management System (ISMS). The most adopted ISMS standard is ISO 27001:2005. The 2005 version of the standard has been updated in 2013 to provide more clarity and more freedom in implementation, based on practical experiences. This paper compares ISO 27001:2005 and the updated 2013 standard, based on Annex A controls. We classify the controls into five categories of data, hardware, software, people and network. All of the controls defined in Annex A, regardless of their objectives, can easily be allocated to at least one of these categories. Classifying the controls to known categories offers an integrated view of the updated standard and presents a suitable guide for evaluating the performance and efficiency of the updated standard.","methods":"Comparative analysis of ISO 27001:2005 and ISO 27001:2013 based on Annex A controls, classified into data, hardware, software, people, and network categories.","results":"All Annex A controls could be allocated to at least one of the five categories, regardless of objective. The categorization provides an integrated view of the updated standard.","conclusions":"Classifying Annex A controls into known categories offers a suitable guide for evaluating the performance and efficiency of ISO 27001:2013. The abstract does not report study limitations.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"Clasificar los controles del Anexo A ayuda a evaluar el desempeño y la eficiencia del SGSI.","tag":"Controles"},{"n":31,"doi":"","authors":"Ţigǎnoaia, B.","year":"2015","journal":"Studies in Informatics and Control","title":"Some aspects regarding the information security management system within organizations - Adopting the ISO/IEC 27001:2013 standard","abstract":"Information security in an organization is one of the most important pillars in achieving organizational objectives. It does not produce profit but it offers the necessary framework for efficiency and efficacy in organization. Information security can be provided in an organization through the implementation and certification of an ISMS-Information Security Management System. This paper presents some aspects regarding the information security management system in an organization and underlines the importance of the adoption of an ISMS and the new elements in ISO/IEC 27001:2013 (new concepts, requirements and changes introduced in the standard). An analysis regarding the correlation between the business risks and features & advantages of the ISO/IEC 27001 standard is presented. There is also proposed a guide for adopting the ISO/IEC 27001:2013 standard, which implies a self-assessment of the organization (which allows to identify where the organization in the ISO/IEC 27001 process is) and strategies (concrete steps and the allocation of resources). The proposed guide will help the organization to understand the relationship between ISO/IEC 27001:2013 and its predecessor ISO/IEC 27001:2005.","methods":"Conceptual paper analyzing the correlation between business risks and the features and advantages of ISO/IEC 27001, and proposing a guide for adopting ISO/IEC 27001:2013 with self-assessment and strategy steps.","results":"The paper identifies new concepts, requirements, and changes introduced in ISO/IEC 27001:2013 and presents a proposed adoption guide based on self-assessment and resource allocation.","conclusions":"Adopting an ISMS and ISO/IEC 27001:2013 is presented as important for organizational security, and the guide is intended to help organizations understand the relationship between the 2013 and 2005 standards.","publisher":"National Institute for R and D in Informatics","open":false,"insight":"Adoptar un SGSI formal clarifica responsabilidades y la transición entre versiones de la norma.","tag":"Gobierno"},{"n":32,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.17559/TV-20200721154801","authors":"Sikman, L.; Latinović, T.; Sarajlić, N.","year":"2022","journal":"Tehnicki Vjesnik","title":"Modelling of Fuzzy Expert System for an Assessment of Security Information Management System UIS (University Information System)","abstract":"Several methodologies based on the international standard ISO/IEC 27001 have been developed for modelling information security management systems within higher education. This paper transformed the ISO/IEC 27001 standard into a questionnaire, which was sent digitally to about 100 universities in Bosnia and Herzegovina, and to the EU, Norway and the USA. The questions are arranged by levels, and the levels have their numerical weights, derived from individual questions in the levels themselves. Otherwise, the questions are asked with Yes or No and thus are reduced to binary variables. The rules necessary for the functioning of the system have been calculated. The fuzzy logic method represents a new approach to the problems of managing complex systems, which is very difficult to describe with a certain mathematical model, as well as in systems with a large number of inputs and outputs where there are unclear interactions. Risk assessment is a major part of the ISMS process. Traditional risk calculation models are based on the application of probability and classical set theory. Here, we have converted the risk assessment into a system rating of 5 to 10 numerically or from five to ten descriptively. We perform fuzzy optimization by finding the values of the input parameters of a complex simulated system, which results in the desired output. We use the fuzzy logic controller to execute fuzzy inference rules from the fuzzy rule database in determining congestion parameters, obtaining warning information and appropriate action. Simulating the situation of an advanced system that evaluates the protection quality of such a system with fuzzy logic, we use MATLAB. The paper combines the original Visual Basic programming language and MATLAB's Fuzzy Toolbox, to solve the complex problem of assessing compliance with the ISO/IEC 27001 standard, as one of the main standards for information systems security modelling. University information systems were used, but it is also applicable to all other information systems. The evaluation has been done for several universities and it has been proven that the system evaluates correctly, but these universities must not be publicly named. There was no such approach in the use of fuzzy logic and on such systems, and that is the originality of this work.","methods":"Questionnaire based on ISO/IEC 27001 sent to about 100 universities in Bosnia and Herzegovina, the EU, Norway, and the USA. Fuzzy logic modelling and optimization were implemented in Visual Basic and MATLAB Fuzzy Toolbox.","results":"The system converted yes/no answers into binary variables, calculated rule sets, and produced a 5- to 10-point security rating. Evaluation on several universities showed the system evaluates correctly.","conclusions":"The fuzzy logic approach was presented as a novel way to assess compliance with ISO/IEC 27001 for university information systems. The authors note that the tested universities cannot be publicly named.","publisher":"Strojarski Facultet","open":true,"insight":"La lógica difusa permite evaluar el cumplimiento de ISO 27001 con grados, no solo con sí o no.","tag":"Medición"},{"n":33,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1108/ICS-10-2024-0269","authors":"Leme, R.D.S.; de Souza Pinto, J.; Zanon, L.G.; ... Anholon, R.","year":"2025","journal":"Information and Computer Security","title":"Information security management: a fuzzy DEMATEL analysis of the new ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls","abstract":"Purpose – This paper aims to analyze the cause-and-effect relationship and the level of influence between the 11 new information security controls proposed by ISO/IEC 27001:2022. Design/methodology/approach – A survey with 22 experts, all chief technology officers, was conducted, to assess the influences between each of these controls by means of a pairwise comparison. Then, the fuzzy Decision-making Trial and Evaluation Laboratory (DEMATEL) method was applied to evaluate the relationships and the influence degree between controls. Findings – The most influential and impacted controls were identified through the Causal Diagram and the Influence Relationship Map. “Threat Intelligence” and “Secure Development” emerged as the main influencing factors, serving as core components in the evaluation system. This finding aligns with the growing emphasis on protecting data stored in cloud services and addressing emerging threats, highlighting their importance in Information Security Management Systems. Furthermore, the central, driving, independent and impactful controls were identified, as well as their priority in allocating resources for their integration and management. This allows for a strategic and efficient distribution of resources, directing them according to the priority and impact of each control on the security system. Originality/value – This study presents an original approach, applying the fuzzy DEMATEL method to analyze the cause-and-effect relationships between the 11 new controls in ISO/IEC 27001:2022. Unlike previous studies, this research reveals the causal structure and prioritization of these updates. By identifying central and influential controls, such as “Threat Intelligence” and “Secure Development, ” this study offers strategic insights for managing these new controls, highlighting those that provide strategic leverage in the practical integration of security measures, as well as which should be targets for resource allocation. This new analytical perspective contributes to a deeper understanding of the evolution of the standard in addressing contemporary cybersecurity challenges, offering practical insights to information security managers, enabling proactive risk management, optimized resource allocation and alignment with regulatory compliance frameworks.","methods":"Survey of 22 chief technology officers using pairwise comparisons, analyzed with fuzzy DEMATEL to assess influence relationships among the 11 new ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls.","results":"Threat Intelligence and Secure Development were the main influencing factors. The study identified the most influential and impacted controls and their priority for resource allocation.","conclusions":"The fuzzy DEMATEL approach revealed the causal structure and prioritization of the new controls, offering strategic guidance for integrating security measures and allocating resources. The abstract does not report explicit limitations.","publisher":"Emerald Publishing","open":false,"insight":"Los controles nuevos de 2022 tienen distinta influencia causal; priorizar los más influyentes optimiza recursos.","tag":"Controles"},{"n":34,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics13163257","authors":"Sabillón, R.; Bermejo-Higuera, J.R.B.; Cano M, J.; ... Sicilia-Montalvo, J.A.S.","year":"2024","journal":"Electronics (Switzerland)","title":"Assessing the Effectiveness of Cyber Domain Controls When Conducting Cybersecurity Audits: Insights from Higher Education Institutions in Canada","abstract":"This study validates a comprehensive cybersecurity audit model through empirical analysis in three higher education institutions in Canada. The research aims to enhance cybersecurity resilience by assessing the effectiveness of cybersecurity controls across diverse educational environments. Given the increasing frequency and sophistication of cyberattacks targeting educational institutions, this research is essential to ensure the protection of sensitive academic and personal data. Data were collected through detailed audits involving system vulnerabilities, compliance with security policies, and incident response management at each institution. The findings underscore the importance of tailored cybersecurity strategies and continuous auditing to mitigate cyber risks in the Canadian higher education sector. This study contributes to the field by validating a versatile audit tool that can be adapted to various institutional contexts, promoting enhanced cybersecurity practices and evaluating the effectiveness of cybersecurity safeguards across the higher education sector in Canada. The results of the audit model validations provide the cybersecurity maturity rating of each institution. Further research is recommended to refine the model and explore its application in other industries and sectors.","methods":"Empirical validation of a cybersecurity audit model across 3 higher education institutions in Canada using detailed audits of vulnerabilities, policy compliance, and incident response management.","results":"Audit model validations produced a cybersecurity maturity rating for each institution and highlighted the need for tailored cybersecurity strategies and continuous auditing.","conclusions":"A versatile audit tool can assess cybersecurity safeguards across higher education institutions in Canada; further refinement and testing in other industries and sectors is recommended.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"Una herramienta de auditoría versátil puede medir salvaguardas entre instituciones, pero debe validarse en más sectores.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":35,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1757-899X/550/1/012020","authors":"Aldya, A.P.; Sutikno, S.; Rosmansyah, Y.","year":"2019","journal":"IOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering","title":"Measuring effectiveness of control of information security management system based on SNI ISO/IEC 27004: 2013 standard","abstract":"One of the keys to the successful implementation of information security management in an organization is the selection and implementation of an information security management system control that is good and in accordance with the needs of the organization, the information security management system control can be adopted based on ISO/IEC 27001: 2013 standard document. To ensure the success of information security controls, it is necessary to measure the effectiveness of each control applied. SNI ISO/IEC 27004: 2013 is a standard that provides guidance on the development and use of measures and measurements to assess the effectiveness of controls and control groups in the information security management system as stated in the ISO/IEC 27001 standard, but to do the measurement process, required objects and measurement attributes and metrics, which are not explained in detail in the ISO ISO/IEC 27004: 2013 standard. This study aims to assist in measuring the effectiveness of information security management control by generating the flow of steps in determining the object and measurement parameters and the metrics used based on the provisions contained in the ISO ISO/IEC 27004: 2013 standard.","methods":"Abstract-only study proposing a step-by-step flow for determining measurement objects, parameters, and metrics to assess information security control effectiveness under SNI ISO/IEC 27004:2013.","results":"The study produced a flow of steps for determining objects, measurement parameters, and metrics for measuring control effectiveness.","conclusions":"SNI ISO/IEC 27004:2013 can guide effectiveness measurement of information security management controls, but the standard lacks detailed objects, attributes, and metrics for the measurement process.","publisher":"Institute of Physics Publishing","open":true,"insight":"ISO 27004 guía la medición de eficacia, aunque exige definir objetos, atributos y métricas concretas.","tag":"Medición"},{"n":36,"doi":"","authors":"Vakhula, O.; Kurii, Y.; Opirskyy, I.; Susukailo, V.","year":"2024","journal":"CEUR Workshop Proceedings","title":"Security-as-Code Concept for Fulfilling ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Requirements","abstract":"This article thoroughly examines and analyzes the latest iteration of the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 standard, outlining the most contemporary requirements for information security management systems. The primary focus of this study centers on the novel control A.8.9, which is dedicated to configuration management. The article delves into the necessity and significance of effectively implementing this control element to ensure a high level of infrastructure security and strategically reduce the risks of security breaches. Particular attention is given to the innovative security approach known as “Secure as Code.” This methodology involves integrating security measures during the early stages of software development to effectively counter potential threats and ensure system resilience from the initial development phase to the operational stage. Serving as a comprehensive analysis and review of current trends in information security, the article not only provides readers with a broad perspective on the standard but also offers specific recommendations for the successful implementation of control A.8.9 of the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 standard. Additionally, practical tips are shared for seamlessly integrating the Secure as Code strategy into development practices. To support the research, an extensive review of literature and articles providing information on the implementation of ISO 27001 and the security-as-code approach were conducted.","methods":"Review article analyzing ISO/IEC 27001:2022, with emphasis on control A.8.9 and the Secure as Code approach. An extensive review of literature and articles on ISO 27001 and security-as-code was conducted.","results":"The paper argues that control A.8.9 and early security integration via Secure as Code can improve infrastructure security and reduce breach risk. It provides recommendations and practical tips for implementing A.8.9 and integrating Secure as Code into development practices.","conclusions":"Secure as Code is presented as a useful strategy for meeting ISO/IEC 27001:2022 requirements, especially A.8.9. Limitation: the abstract reports a review and recommendations rather than empirical validation.","publisher":"CEUR-WS","open":false,"insight":"Security-as-Code ayuda a cumplir 27001:2022, en especial la gestión de configuración A.8.9.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":37,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cose.2026.105216","authors":"Cabrillo Bartolomé, J.I.; Vega, M.Á.N.; Palacio Hoz, A.","year":"2027","journal":"Computers and Security","title":"An open-source-based Information Security Management System for scientific computing: A reference implementation and case study","abstract":"Scientific computing centres combine HPC, Grid, Cloud, storage, and federated services, creating heterogeneous environments in which information-security requirements must be translated into operational processes and technical mechanisms. This paper presents an implementation methodology and real-world case study of a predominantly open-source-based Information Security Management System (ISMS) at the Institute of Physics of Cantabria (IFCA). The methodology establishes explicit traceability between key challenges associated with securing research infrastructures, including risk management, access control, data protection, and regulatory compliance. The reference implementation supports compliance with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and the Spanish National Security Framework (ENS) through open-source technologies for identity and access management, configuration control, monitoring, log management, vulnerability management, backup, incident handling, documentation, and collaboration. The evaluation combines control coverage, implementation maturity, operational indicators, documentary evidence, and independent audit results. Of the 126 applicable entries in the combined ISO/IEC 27001–ENS control register, 120 had an identified implementation mechanism, resulting in a control coverage of 95.2% and an estimated implementation maturity of 86.7%. Successful certification and subsequent surveillance activities provide independent evidence of conformity within the defined scope. The case study shows that open-source technologies can support a substantial part of a scientific-computing ISMS, emphasising the importance of integrating security practices into scientific workflows, supporting operational consistency and providing independent evidence of conformity within the defined scope. The proposed methodology, together with the mapping between controls, operational capabilities, and supporting technologies, provides a reference that may be adapted by other research institutions, although further validation in additional computing centres is required.","methods":"Implementation-oriented case study at IFCA using a reproducible control-to-capability-to-process-to-tool methodology for an open-source-based ISMS. Analysis used the Statement of Applicability, risk-treatment plan, asset inventory, procedures, operational records, control tests, and internal/external audits against ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and ENS.","results":"Of 126 applicable control entries, 120 had an implementation mechanism, yielding 95.2% control coverage and 86.7% estimated maturity. Initial critical risks fell from 37 assets to 4 residual critical risks; 82 covered controls were fully implemented and 34 operational with gaps.","conclusions":"The ISMS aligned with ISO/IEC 27001 and ENS was successfully certified, and open-source tools supported much of the required operational capability. Main limitations are dependence on specialized personnel and the need for validation in additional centers; certification reflects conformity within a defined scope, not universal transferability.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":true,"insight":"Un SGSI con herramientas open source puede certificarse; depende de personal especializado y de un alcance bien definido.","tag":"Pymes"},{"n":38,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.14569/IJACSA.2023.0140468","authors":"Putra, A.P.; Soewito, B.","year":"2023","journal":"International Journal of Advanced Computer Science and Applications","title":"Integrated Methodology for Information Security Risk Management using ISO 27005:2018 and NIST SP 800-30 for Insurance Sector","abstract":"The development of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) in the Industrial Revolution 4.0 era shows very fast and disruptive developments that encourage increased use of Information Technology (IT) services within organizations. However, there is a risk of creating vulnerabilities and threats to owned information systems. Plans and strategies are required to implement information security risk management to address vulnerabilities in threat events. This research is a case study of the Enterprise Resource Planning System in the Insurance Sector. The proposed methodologies for integrating information security risk management using ISO/IEC 27005:2018 as a risk management framework and NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 as guidance for risk assessments. The risk evaluation stage is the process of comparing the results of the risk analysis with the risk criteria to then determine whether the risk rating is acceptable or tolerable. For risk treatment and control using the ISO/IEC 27002:2022 framework.","methods":"Case study of an Enterprise Resource Planning system in the insurance sector using ISO/IEC 27005:2018 as the risk management framework, NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 for risk assessment, and ISO/IEC 27002:2022 for risk treatment and control.","results":"The paper proposes an integrated information security risk management methodology and describes risk evaluation as comparing risk analysis results with risk criteria to judge whether risk is acceptable or tolerable.","conclusions":"The integrated framework is presented as a way to manage information security risks in insurance-sector ERP systems. No explicit limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Science and Information Organization","open":true,"insight":"Integrar ISO 27005 y NIST 800-30 estructura el tratamiento de riesgos en sistemas ERP.","tag":"Riesgo"},{"n":39,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3844/jcssp.2026.778.786","authors":"Alfitrah, D.A.; Leegowo, N.","year":"2026","journal":"Journal of Computer Science","title":"Risk Assessment of Healthcare Information Systems in Indonesian Regional Government Hospitals Using ISO 27001:2022","abstract":"The growing number of cyber-attacks targeting the healthcare sector, particularly Indonesian regional government hospitals, reflects the absence of a structured information security management system. Issues such as shared account usage, lack of staff security training, and undocumented incident reporting present serious risks to patient data. This study aims to assess the current state of information security in a government hospital using the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 standard and to propose mitigation measures based on Annex A controls. The assessment was conducted using the ISO 27001 framework and methodology. A qualitative case study approach was adopted, with data collected through semi-structured interviews, direct observations, and document analysis. The evaluation followed the Plan Do Check Act (PDCA) cycle and ISO 27005 risk assessment matrix, scoring each risk based on likelihood and impact. The results show that out of eight identified risk categories, four were classified as high namely, access management, information security policy, security awareness training, and system backup management while the rest were categorized as medium. A gap analysis indicated that many of these risks were not supported by effective controls. Recommendations include policy updates, regular training, formalized incident reporting, and annual security audits. These findings highlight the urgent need for systematic ISMS implementation to improve cybersecurity resilience and safeguard patient information in public healthcare institutions.","methods":"Qualitative case study of a government hospital using ISO/IEC 27001:2022, with semi-structured interviews, direct observations, and document analysis. Risk evaluation used the PDCA cycle and ISO 27005 matrix.","results":"Eight risk categories were identified; four were high risk (access management, information security policy, security awareness training, and system backup management) and the rest were medium. Gap analysis found many risks lacked effective controls.","conclusions":"Systematic ISMS implementation is urgently needed to improve cybersecurity resilience and protect patient information. Recommended actions include policy updates, regular training, formal incident reporting, and annual security audits.","publisher":"Science Publications","open":true,"insight":"Los hospitales necesitan políticas, formación, reporte de incidentes y auditorías anuales para proteger al paciente.","tag":"Salud"},{"n":40,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-52856-0_1","authors":"Kanter-Ramirez, C.A.; López-Leyva, J.A.; Beltran-Rocha, L.; Ferková, D.","year":"2020","journal":"Lecture Notes of the Institute for Computer Sciences, Social-Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering, LNICST","title":"Framework for the optimal design of an information system to diagnostic the enterprise security level and management the information risk based on ISO/IEC-27001","abstract":"This paper presents the framework for the optimized development of a digital platform based on ISO/IEC-27001 with the objective of making an initial diagnosis regarding the informatics security level in any company. In addition, the optimization process considers that the diagnostic results should be clear and direct, to making possible the fast security risk mitigation. In particular, the optimization process is based on the analysis of a conventional Management Information System framework in order to propose a novel customized framework for ISO/IEC-27001 applications. Thus, an optimized Management Information System is proposed which is the basis of the optimized digital platform. As preliminary results, the reduction of needed elements for the initial diagnosis for the informatics security promotes the simplicity of the application and thus, increases the possibility of applying the ISO/IEC-27001 to a greater amount of users, which means that it is promoted cybersecurity.","methods":"Framework proposal paper based on analysis of a conventional Management Information System to develop a customized ISO/IEC-27001 digital platform for initial security diagnosis. No formal empirical study, sample, or statistical analysis is described in the abstract.","results":"Preliminary results indicate that reducing the elements needed for initial security diagnosis simplifies the application and may increase the number of users able to apply ISO/IEC-27001.","conclusions":"The proposed optimized framework is intended to enable clear, rapid security-risk mitigation and broader ISO/IEC-27001 adoption. The abstract does not report limitations.","publisher":"Springer Nature","open":false,"insight":"Un diagnóstico de madurez basado en ISO 27001 permite mitigar riesgos con rapidez.","tag":"Riesgo"},{"n":41,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICoCICs68032.2025.11384072","authors":"Harahap, M.R.R.; Candiwan, C.","year":"2025","journal":"Proceedings - 2025 IEEE 2nd International Conference on Cryptography, Informatics, and Cybersecurity, ICoCICs 2025","title":"Risk Mitigation of Security Information Through Optimization Risk Assessment Based on ISO 27005:2022 & NIST SP 800-30 Revision 1 Standards at XYZ Company","abstract":"In today's digital world, institutions confront increasing problems in protecting their information assets from developing cyber threats. This research uses ISO/IEC 27005:2022 as a framework for structured risk assessment and NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 for systematic risk measurement at Company XYZ. ISO/IEC 27005:2022 offered a broad theoretical base, whereas NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 supplemented it with more particular criteria, allowing for a more complete risk assessment specific to the education sector. To analyze IT service practices and vulnerabilities, a qualitative technique was used, which included observations and interviews. The evaluation found 9 risk scenarios, 6 of which were classed as high and 3 as medium, including major assaults such as distributed denial of service (DDoS), SQL injection, and unauthorized document disclosure. To mitigate these risks, 9 control suggestions were developed based on ISO 27002:2022, which included periodic security hardening, online filtering, and stronger document categorization procedures. These strategies seek to eliminate residual risks while ensuring company continuity. The findings not only provide an intellectual contribution by incorporating ISO/IEC and NIST frameworks into a bespoke assessment model, but they also provide practical advice for XYZ Company on how to strengthen security governance, comply with international standards, and proactively prevent future cybersecurity risks.","methods":"Qualitative risk assessment at Company XYZ using ISO/IEC 27005:2022 and NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1, based on observations and interviews.","results":"Identified 9 risk scenarios: 6 high and 3 medium, including DDoS, SQL injection, and unauthorized document disclosure. Developed 9 mitigation controls based on ISO 27002:2022.","conclusions":"The framework-based assessment supports stronger security governance, standards compliance, and proactive cybersecurity risk prevention at XYZ Company.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"La evaluación basada en estándares fortalece el gobierno y la prevención proactiva de incidentes.","tag":"Riesgo"},{"n":42,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CITSM50537.2020.9268845","authors":"Putra, S.; Gunawan, M.; Falach Sobri, A.; ... Saepudin, D.","year":"2020","journal":"2020 8th International Conference on Cyber and IT Service Management, CITSM 2020","title":"Information Security Risk Management Analysis Using ISO 27005: 2011 for the Telecommunication Company","abstract":"The implementation of information security risk management in the telecommunications company is carried out to systematically manage potential losses arising from opportunities and consequences in the information security business process. This implementation uses ISO 27005 in relation to the requirements of ISO 27001 which has control objectives that can be used as a basis for controlling risk. The maintenance and inspection program implemented in the telecommunications company has not considered the risk aspects in the decision-making process so that the problem has resulted in not optimal risk handling and control. The purpose of this study is to determine the selection of the right control object for the basis of a suitable risk control program to support the requirements of the ISO 27001 information security management system standard. This study begins with context establishment, followed by risk identification, risk analysis, risk evaluation, and risk treatment. The sampling technique used purposive sampling and snowball sampling. Data collection was obtained from the management and processed with computer-assisted qualitative data analysis software NVivo 10. This study produces 26 impact scenarios for the highest rank category and 12 impact scenarios as the top priority. Based on the results of risk evaluation, eight ISO 27001 control objectives need to be considered in order to support information security. The results of this study can be used for planning decisions and risk control work programs.","methods":"Case study using ISO 27005:2011 aligned with ISO 27001; context establishment, risk identification, analysis, evaluation, and treatment. Data came from management, using purposive and snowball sampling and NVivo 10 for qualitative analysis.","results":"Produced 26 impact scenarios in the highest rank category and 12 impact scenarios as top priority. Risk evaluation identified eight ISO 27001 control objectives that should be considered for information security support.","conclusions":"The selected ISO 27001 control objectives can support information security planning and risk control work programs. The abstract does not state major limitations.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"Los objetivos de control de ISO 27001 orientan la planificación y los programas de tratamiento.","tag":"Riesgo"},{"n":43,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.12913/22998624/185162","authors":"El Fray, I.E.; Wiliński, A.","year":"2024","journal":"Advances in Science and Technology Research Journal","title":"Modifications of the Formal Risk Analysis and Assessment for the Information System Security","abstract":"In the article, a modification of formal model of risk analysis (FoMRA) was proposed. The modified FoMRA1 method takes into account the guidelines of ISO/IEC 27001 and ISO/IEC 27005 standards. The applied modification and abstraction by resources and security controls (also called countermeasures) significantly shortened the time of risk weight calculation in comparison with the MEHARI method. An attempt was also made to further reduce the time of risk analysis using agents collecting information and data from various network nodes, from operating systems and devices, and additional agents containing information on reports on security procedures, security services, security management and organizational activities related to the information systems (mainte-nance, insurance, outsourcing contracts, etc.) and transfer it to the local FoMRA1 database. The obtained results indicate that the proposed method together with agents installed in various nodes enable a quick reaction to the system threats and prevention of their impacts (quasi-real-time security monitoring system).","methods":"Proposed modification of the formal model of risk analysis (FoMRA1) based on ISO/IEC 27001 and ISO/IEC 27005, with agents collecting data from network nodes, operating systems, devices, and security-management sources.","results":"The modified FoMRA1 significantly shortened risk-weight calculation time versus the MEHARI method and enabled near-real-time security monitoring.","conclusions":"The proposed method with distributed agents allows quick reaction to system threats and prevention of their impacts. It is presented as a quasi-real-time security monitoring system.","publisher":"Politechnika Lubelska","open":true,"insight":"Agentes distribuidos permiten reaccionar casi en tiempo real a las amenazas del sistema.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":44,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app16126260","authors":"Vasovic, D.; Janackovic, G.; Vranjanac, Ž.; ... Vasovic, B.","year":"2026","journal":"Applied Sciences (Switzerland)","title":"Enhancing CIA Triad—Confidentiality, Integrity and Availability in Educational Information Systems Through Next-Generation ISO/IEC 27001:2022-Aligned Security Model","abstract":"Featured Application: This study can be applied to strengthening the security architecture of digital education platforms by integrating next-generation security protocols into ISO/IEC 27001-aligned governance models. It provides guidance elements for educational institutions seeking to enhance the CIA triad in increasingly interconnected learning environments. Educational information systems have evolved into highly interconnected digital landscapes that support learning management platforms, student information systems, institutional repositories, and online assessment environments. As these systems increasingly operate across cloud infrastructures and mobile devices, ensuring the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA Triad) of educational data is critical for safeguarding institutional operations and maintaining trust in digital education services. This paper investigates how next-generation security protocols, such as adaptive multi-factor authentication and advanced access control and data protection mechanisms, can reinforce ISO/IEC 27001:2022 requirements within contemporary educational information systems. The analysis maps emerging protocol capabilities to relevant new ISO/IEC 27001:2022 control domains, illustrating how they mitigate threats associated with unauthorized access, data manipulation, and service disruption. The proposed framework is further supported by an implementation-oriented mapping and an illustrative operational architecture that demonstrates the feasibility of translating prioritized security determinants into practical mechanisms. The FAHP analysis identifies access control mechanisms, backup and recovery, and data validation as the three highest-weighted determinants, with aggregate weights of 0.061, 0.059, and 0.057, respectively. These determinants are translated into a determinant-driven Security Operationalization Matrix that connects ISO/IEC 27001:2022 control domains, CIA dimensions, and technology recommendations, and is complemented by implementation feasibility considerations tailored to the budgetary, infrastructural, and resource constraints characteristic of educational institutions. Based on the prioritization results and conceptual operationalization, the proposed integrative approach provides a structured and progressively adoptable foundation for CIA-oriented security governance in digital educational environments.","methods":"Conceptual analysis mapping next-generation security protocols to ISO/IEC 27001:2022 control domains in educational information systems, supported by an FAHP prioritization and an illustrative operational architecture.","results":"FAHP ranked access control mechanisms (0.061), backup and recovery (0.059), and data validation (0.057) as the top three determinants. The framework maps these determinants into a Security Operationalization Matrix linking ISO/IEC 27001:2022 domains, CIA dimensions, and technology recommendations.","conclusions":"The proposed approach offers a structured, progressively adoptable foundation for CIA-oriented security governance in digital educational environments. It is framed as feasible for institutions with budgetary, infrastructural, and resource constraints.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"Un modelo alineado a 27001:2022 y adoptable por etapas mejora confidencialidad, integridad y disponibilidad.","tag":"Educación"},{"n":45,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-99353-4_24","authors":"Quispe, G.O.; Zuloaga, C.K.; Castañeda, P.S.","year":"2026","journal":"Communications in Computer and Information Science","title":"Mitigating Information Leakage in Tech-Sector SMEs: Implementing ISO 27001:2022 for Comprehensive Security","abstract":"This paper presents a model for implementing an Information Security Management System (ISMS) based on ISO 27001:2022 tailored to the needs of small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in the technology sector in Lima Metropolitana. The model focuses on mitigating data leakage, a critical issue exacerbated by the increasing digitization of business operations. The proposed framework integrates controls from ISO 27001 aligned with NIST SP 800-53 to enhance information security practices. Results from applying the model to two technology SMEs indicate that one company (Company A) achieved a 94.44% Critical Control Implementation Index (IICC), a 70% Critical Vulnerability Resolution Rate (TRVC), and an 85% Policy Compliance Rate (TCPS), while the second company (Company B) achieved significantly lower rates of 50%, 40%, and 60%, respectively. These findings highlight both strengths in technological controls and weaknesses in organizational security management. This research contributes to the field by providing a practical, scalable approach for SMEs to enhance their information security posture, addressing both human and technological factors.","methods":"Model development and application of an ISMS based on ISO 27001:2022, integrating NIST SP 800-53 controls, in two technology SMEs in Lima Metropolitana.","results":"Company A achieved a 94.44% Critical Control Implementation Index, 70% Critical Vulnerability Resolution Rate, and 85% Policy Compliance Rate; Company B achieved 50%, 40%, and 60%, respectively.","conclusions":"The model is practical and scalable for improving SME information security, with stronger technological controls than organizational security management.","publisher":"Springer Nature","open":false,"insight":"En pymes tecnológicas los controles técnicos suelen superar a la gestión organizacional: hay que equilibrar ambas dimensiones.","tag":"Pymes"},{"n":46,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10207-026-01252-y","authors":"Islam, S.; Haider, A.; Ahmad, F.B.; ... Mazhar, T.","year":"2026","journal":"International Journal of Information Security","title":"Automating cybersecurity audits: analyzing the impact of the ISO27K1 Toolkit on SME compliance and risk management","abstract":"In today's digitally connected world, safeguarding information is paramount for organizations. With the increasing complexity of digital systems and the rising number of cyber threats, information security and cybersecurity risk management has become a necessary function for today’s business. Auditing is in place to assess and mitigate risks of information security. However, the audit process can be intimidating and complex. It involves a broad spectrum of methods that analyze existing controls in a variety of information security and cybersecurity areas. To reduce risks and enhance compliance with standards, the plan has corrective actions for all controls in the checklist. Although most ISMSs can co-exist, most organizations have a single ISMS with their respective tools and processes. This setup may limit flexibility and diversity, making the overall result of audit assessments less effective. Moreover, using outdated tools and processes makes it difficult to provide answers to questions about cybersecurity management and may cause gaps in fulfilling requirements. This gap makes organizations susceptible to threats and possible non-compliance with regulations. In general, SMEs have different auditing requirements from large-scale businesses, and existing ISMSs do not support them comprehensively at the controlling level. In short, existing ISMSs are not suitable for auditing SMEs. To overcome these issues, we have developed an end-to-end system that encompasses all aspects of cybersecurity auditing within one platform: the ISO27K1 Toolkit, designed to meet user preferences. Its advanced web interface has improved automated information security auditing. The system is flexible and dynamic, supporting multiple auditing methods and the integration of predefined control checklists and corresponding mitigation actions. This paper describes the development of the ISO27K1 Toolkit, designed based on ISO 27001:2022 controls and future-proofed for compatibility with the NIST-CSF and PCI DSS standards. The ISO27K1 Toolkit automates critical security assessment tasks, improving user experience and supporting audit processes while promoting compliance, efficiency, and proactive risk management. Large-scale business organizations and SMEs can take advantage of the relational database of the ISO 27K1 Toolkit, which maintains the latest auditing and intervention data. The project was implemented among 35 SMEs and focused on information security audits and system assessments according to ISO 27001:2022. This document presents the system architecture and design, as well as the results of global testing.","methods":"System development paper describing the ISO27K1 Toolkit, a web-based platform built around ISO 27001:2022 controls and compatible with NIST-CSF and PCI DSS. Implemented among 35 SMEs, with system architecture, design, and global testing reported.","results":"The toolkit automated information security auditing and critical assessment tasks, improving user experience and supporting compliance, efficiency, and proactive risk management. The abstract reports improved auditing and successful global testing, but no quantitative outcomes are given.","conclusions":"ISO27K1 is presented as a flexible, dynamic auditing platform that can support SMEs and larger organizations in meeting cybersecurity audit and compliance needs. A limitation is that the abstract provides no detailed performance metrics beyond global testing.","publisher":"Springer Nature","open":true,"insight":"Automatizar auditorías facilita el cumplimiento en pymes, pero faltan métricas detalladas de desempeño.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":47,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0015076700004103","authors":"Andrade, M.; Almeida, J.R.; Oliveira, J.L.","year":"2026","journal":"Proceedings of the International Conference on Security and Cryptography","title":"Managing Cybersecurity Compliance with Structured Guidance and Integrated Audit Support","abstract":"As cybersecurity regulations such as ISO/IEC 27001 and the NIS2 Directive continue to expand in scope and complexity, organizations face growing challenges in translating regulatory obligations into actionable security policies and audit-ready evidence. Conventional compliance approaches rely on manual interpretation of regulatory texts, fragmented documentation repositories, and ad hoc audit preparation, introducing operational bottlenecks and exposing organizations to non-compliance risks. This paper presents a compliance management platform that operationalizes regulatory requirements through structured, expert-guided control implementation. It combines NLP extraction with human-supervised annotation to convert regulatory texts into machine-readable frameworks, enabling multi-framework management (ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and NIS2), control mapping, evidence tracking, and role-based audit workflows. In a task-based usability study with twelve participants, the platform scored 83.3 on the System Usability Scale (SUS), rated “excellent,” indicating that embedded guidance can reduce expertise barriers in cybersecurity compliance management.","methods":"Compliance management platform using NLP extraction and human-supervised annotation to convert regulatory texts into machine-readable frameworks, with multi-framework management, control mapping, evidence tracking, and role-based audit workflows. Evaluated in a task-based usability study with 12 participants.","results":"The platform scored 83.3 on the System Usability Scale (SUS), rated excellent. Embedded guidance reduced expertise barriers in cybersecurity compliance management.","conclusions":"Embedded guidance can reduce expertise barriers in cybersecurity compliance management. The abstract does not report specific limitations.","publisher":"Science and Technology Publications, Lda","open":true,"insight":"La guía integrada reduce la barrera de experiencia en la gestión del cumplimiento.","tag":"Auditoría"},{"n":48,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cose.2025.104774","authors":"Mirtsch, M.; Pohlisch, J.; Blind, K.","year":"2026","journal":"Computers and Security","title":"Certification as a compensation mechanism for weak regulation? Exploring the diffusion of the international standard ISO/IEC 27001 for information security management","abstract":"Safeguarding information security has become a key managerial responsibility. The standard “Information security, cybersecurity and privacy protection - Information security management systems - Requirements” (ISO/IEC 27001) specifies the requirements for establishing, implementing, maintaining, and continually improving an information security management system (ISMS). It provides a systematic approach to managing sensitive information, ensuring its confidentiality, integrity, and availability through risk management and security controls. While the number of valid certifications has grown significantly over time, adoption rates vary widely across countries. Drawing on signaling theory, we present the first comprehensive global study of ISO/IEC 27001 diffusion, with a particular focus on the influence of regulatory frameworks and international trade. Based on regression analyses covering 128 countries having implemented ISO/IEC 27001 between 2006 and 2017, our findings suggest that organizations may use ISO/IEC 27001 certification as a signaling mechanism, especially in environments with less stringent regulatory frameworks.","methods":"Macro-level panel study of 128 countries using ISO Survey data from 2006–2017. Fixed-effects Poisson regression with cluster-robust errors tested the effects of ICT regulation, exports, and ISO 9001 diffusion on ISO/IEC 27001 certificates.","results":"Less stringent ICT regulation was negatively associated with ISO/IEC 27001 diffusion, while exports and ISO 9001 diffusion were positively associated. Robustness checks using alternative ICT regulation measures, imputation, PCA, and fixed-effects negative binomial models confirmed the main findings.","conclusions":"ISO/IEC 27001 appears to function as a signaling mechanism and compensates for weak ICT regulation, especially in export-oriented contexts. Limitations include reliance on voluntarily reported ISO Survey data, no sector-specific analysis, no uncertified implementations, and limited causal inference.","publisher":"Elsevier","open":true,"insight":"ISO 27001 actúa como señal y compensa regulaciones TIC débiles, sobre todo en economías exportadoras.","tag":"Legal"},{"n":49,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/computers15060364","authors":"Zulfigarzada, S.; Gadirli, A.; Karimov, J.; ... Ažubalis, M.","year":"2026","journal":"Computers","title":"Cybersecurity Requirements and Certification Standards in Industrial Automation Systems: A Systematic Review","abstract":"Industrial automation systems are increasingly cyber-physical, interconnected, and software-dependent, which expands both their operational capability and their cybersecurity exposure. This article reports a systematic literature review, conducted following the PRISMA 2020 guidelines, of cybersecurity requirements and certification standards in industrial automation, with emphasis on Industrial Control Systems (ICS), Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA), Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs), and Industry 4.0 contexts. From 3570 records identified across five academic databases, 75 studies were retained after duplicate removal, title and abstract screening, and full-text eligibility assessment. The included studies were analyzed along three dimensions: cybersecurity requirements, standards and certification, and application context. Quantitative synthesis shows that network segmentation, intrusion detection, secure communication, access control, lifecycle security, and safety–security coordination are the six most frequently emphasized requirement categories, and that ISA/IEC 62443, ISO/IEC 27001, NIST SP 800-82, and NERC-CIP are the four dominant certification frameworks. The review identifies four critical gaps between technical cybersecurity requirements and certification practice and proposes an integrated mapping framework linking requirement categories, standards, and application contexts. The findings indicate that effective industrial cybersecurity assurance depends on a layered compliance architecture rather than on dependence on any single framework.","methods":"Systematic literature review following PRISMA 2020. 3570 records from five academic databases were screened, and 75 studies were retained and analyzed across requirements, standards/certification, and application context.","results":"Network segmentation, intrusion detection, secure communication, access control, lifecycle security, and safety–security coordination were the six most emphasized requirement categories. ISA/IEC 62443, ISO/IEC 27001, NIST SP 800-82, and NERC-CIP were the four dominant certification frameworks, and four gaps between requirements and certification practice were identified.","conclusions":"Effective industrial cybersecurity assurance depends on a layered compliance architecture rather than any single framework. The review proposes an integrated mapping framework, but the findings are based on the reviewed literature rather than direct empirical testing.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"La aseguración industrial requiere una arquitectura de cumplimiento por capas, no un único marco.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":50,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1080/07366981.2026.2681907","authors":"Varshney, P.K.; Wadhwani, G.K.; Kumar, R.; Kumar, R.","year":"2026","journal":"EDPACS","title":"Risk-based information systems auditing for disaster recovery and business continuity","abstract":"Disaster recovery and business continuity management (BCM) have consequently become central concerns of corporate governance, yet the assurance practices used to verify continuity arrangements have not kept pace: traditional information systems audits remain largely checklist-driven, periodic, and compliance-oriented, frequently confirming that documents exist without confirming that an organization could genuinely recover. This paper examines how a risk-based approach to IS auditing can strengthen assurance over disaster recovery and BCM. Using a qualitative design science strategy, it synthesizes the professional literature, including COBIT 2019, ISO 22301:2019, ISO/IEC 27031, NIST SP 80034 Rev. 1, and the ISACA ITAF, and develops an integrated six-phase risk-based audit framework that links an audit universe to a structured continuity risk assessment, maps recovery controls to identified risks, prescribes evidence-based testing rather than documentation review, and embeds continuous monitoring. The analysis shows that risk-based auditing concentrates scarce audit effort where failure would harm the organization most, shifts the central question from whether a plan exists to whether it would work, and yields findings expressed in the language of business impact. Persistent challenges such as weak business impact analysis, untested procedures, cloud concentration, ransomware, and operational resilience regulation and practical recommendations are also discussed.","methods":"Qualitative design science strategy synthesizing professional literature, including COBIT 2019, ISO 22301:2019, ISO/IEC 27031, NIST SP 80034 Rev. 1, and ISACA ITAF. Developed an integrated six-phase risk-based audit framework.","results":"The framework links an audit universe to continuity risk assessment, maps recovery controls to risks, and emphasizes evidence-based testing and continuous monitoring. Risk-based auditing focuses effort where failure would harm the organization most and reframes assurance around whether recovery would work.","conclusions":"Risk-based IS auditing can strengthen assurance over disaster recovery and business continuity by targeting business impact rather than documentation compliance. Persistent challenges include weak business impact analysis, untested procedures, cloud concentration, ransomware, and operational resilience regulation.","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","open":false,"insight":"Auditar continuidad por impacto de negocio exige probar procedimientos; documentar no basta.","tag":"Continuidad"},{"n":51,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1080/07366981.2026.2677209","authors":"Hajam, A.A.; Meshram, J.; Sania, U.; ... Ali, S.A.","year":"2026","journal":"EDPACS","title":"Predictive cyber risk management using artificial intelligence: Implications for audit and compliance","abstract":"The increasing sophistication of cyber threats has exposed the limitations of traditional cyber risk management approaches that rely on reactive controls and periodic assessments. Simultaneously, audit and compliance functions continue to operate in a largely retrospective manner, creating a disconnect between emerging cyber risks and governance mechanisms. This paper proposes an AI-Driven Predictive Cyber Risk Management (AIPCRM) framework that integrates predictive cybersecurity intelligence with audit and compliance operations. The framework combines machine learning techniques, including behavioral analytics, anomaly detection, and threat forecasting, with enterprise governance, risk, and compliance systems to enable proactive risk identification and continuous monitoring. A key contribution is the Explainable Predictive Risk Index (EPRI), which converts AI-generated risk outputs into interpretable and auditable metrics for governance stakeholders. The framework further aligns predictive outputs with established standards such as COBIT 2019, ISO/IEC 27001:2022, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, enabling real-time compliance validation and continuous auditing. A scenario-based illustration demonstrates how predictive intelligence can identify potential cyber incidents before exploitation and trigger automated audit responses. The study highlights improved risk visibility, faster governance response, and enhanced compliance effectiveness while addressing challenges related to explainability, model governance, and regulatory acceptance.","methods":"Proposes an AI-Driven Predictive Cyber Risk Management (AIPCRM) framework using machine learning, behavioral analytics, anomaly detection, and threat forecasting aligned with COBIT 2019, ISO/IEC 27001:2022, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework. Includes a scenario-based illustration rather than an empirical study.","results":"Scenario-based illustration shows predictive intelligence can identify potential cyber incidents before exploitation and trigger automated audit responses. The framework is described as improving risk visibility, governance response speed, and compliance effectiveness.","conclusions":"AI-driven predictive cyber risk management may enable proactive risk identification and continuous auditing for audit and compliance functions. Key challenges remain explainability, model governance, and regulatory acceptance.","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","open":false,"insight":"La IA puede anticipar riesgos y habilitar auditoría continua, con retos de explicabilidad y gobierno del modelo.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":52,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/C358072.2023.10436270","authors":"Hernandez, L.H.; Escobar, Y.; Acosta, F.; ... Prasetya, A.","year":"2023","journal":"1st IEEE Colombian Caribbean Conference, C3 2023","title":"Preparation of the Information Security Management System Implementation Based on the NTC-ISO-IEC 27001:2013 Standard at the IUB University Institution","abstract":"This research was carried out to generate an implementation plan for the information security management system based on the NTC-ISO-IEC 27001:2013 standard at the IUB university institution. The connotation of security has been extended over time due to technological advances and the introduction of new information systems, which simultaneously generate new security challenges. Likewise, the instruments to guarantee the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information have become a fundamental strategy to ensure the security of public and private organizations. The preparation of this plan includes the methodological cycle, where they indicate a series of phases and their corresponding activities to implement the ISMS ISO 27001:2013, with procedural characteristics that support the entire implementation process from beginning to end, facilitating due process and continuity. The result of this cycle will be a plan with a schedule of activities so that the organization links all the personnel around compliance with the standard, raising awareness regarding the importance of information security and the development of activities in phases that, within the stipulated times, will be able to have the ISMS fully operational.","methods":"Implementation-plan study for an ISMS based on NTC-ISO-IEC 27001:2013 at IUB University. It uses a methodological cycle with phases and activities to guide the implementation process end to end.","results":"A plan with a schedule of activities was produced to align personnel with the standard and support phased implementation of the ISMS.","conclusions":"The plan is intended to raise awareness of information security and make the ISMS fully operational within the stipulated times. No limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"Un plan de implantación por fases moviliza al personal y hace operativo el SGSI.","tag":"Educación"},{"n":53,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.32397/tesea.vol5.n2.635","authors":"Hernandez, L.; Pranolo, A.; Wibawa, A.P.","year":"2024","journal":"Transactions on Energy Systems and Engineering Applications","title":"Implementation plan of the information security management system based on the NTC-ISO-IEC 27001:2013 standard and security risk analysis. Case study: Higher education institution","abstract":"This research was carried out to generate an implementation plan for the information security management system based on the NTC-ISO-IEC 27001:2013 standard and security risk analysis at the IUB university institution. The connotation of security has been extended over time due to technological advances and the introduction of new information systems, which simultaneously generate new security challenges. Likewise, the instruments to guarantee the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information have become a fundamental strategy to ensure the security of public and private organizations. The preparation of this plan includes the methodological cycle, where they indicate a series of phases and their corresponding activities to implement the ISMS ISO 27001:2013, with procedural characteristics that support the entire implementation process from beginning to end, facilitating due process and continuity. Likewise, the information security risk plan is analyzed, and significant progress has been made. The result of this cycle will be a plan with a schedule of activities so that the organization links all the personnel around compliance with the standard, raising awareness regarding the importance of information security and the development of activities in phases that, within the stipulated times, will be able to have the ISMS fully operational.","methods":"Case study at IUB university institution using a methodological cycle with phases and activities to implement the NTC-ISO-IEC 27001:2013 ISMS and conduct security risk analysis.","results":"An implementation plan and activity schedule were developed, and the information security risk plan showed significant progress.","conclusions":"The planned phased implementation is intended to mobilize personnel around standard compliance and make the ISMS fully operational within the stipulated time. No limitations are stated in the abstract.","publisher":"Universidad Tecnologica de Bolivar","open":true,"insight":"La implantación por fases con análisis de riesgo alinea a la institución con la norma.","tag":"Educación"},{"n":54,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IC-ICTRuDev50538.2021.9656529","authors":"Putra, D.S.K.; Tistiyani, S.; Sunaringtyas, S.U.","year":"2021","journal":"Proceeding - 2021 2nd International Conference on ICT for Rural Development, IC-ICTRuDev 2021","title":"The Use of ISO/IEC 27001 Family of Standards in Regulatory Requirements in Some Countries","abstract":"ISO/IEC 27001 is an international standard with contain an information security management systems (ISMS) framework as a guidance for auditor and implementer to comply the ISMS standard. The application of the ISO/IEC 27001 standard can help a country or organization in building and maintaining an information security management system (ISMS). In addition to protecting data and information, ISO/ IEC27001 can also manage information security risks to increase the trust of relevant stakeholders. Nowadays, there is no structured information regarding the use of standards in information security. In Indonesia, the ISO/IEC 27001 family is widely used for implementing information security in the cyber field. Therefore, there is a need for a comparative study of the use of ISO/IEC 27001 in Indonesia and other countries. There is a need for details regarding the implementation of the ISO/IEC 27001 family in various countries for academics or implementers. This study aims to provide information to the government, information security experts, academics, and implementers regarding which countries are implementing the ISO/IEC 27001 family in the implementation of their information security policy requirements also compared to the GCI rank. Our comparative analysis has shown that Sweden uses ISO/IEC 27001 family the most, the second is the United Kingdom and the third is Malaysia. So, the ISO/IEC 27001 family of standards use is affecting the GCI rank which lies in the legal measures.","methods":"Comparative analysis of ISO/IEC 27001 family use in Indonesia and other countries, comparing regulatory requirements and GCI rank. No formal search or study design is described in the abstract.","results":"Sweden used the ISO/IEC 27001 family the most, followed by the United Kingdom and Malaysia. The study reports that ISO/IEC 27001 use is affecting GCI rank in legal measures.","conclusions":"ISO/IEC 27001 family standards are important for implementing information security policy requirements and may influence GCI legal-measures ranking. The abstract does not state specific study limitations.","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","open":false,"insight":"La familia ISO 27001 influye en requisitos regulatorios y en el ranking legal de ciberseguridad.","tag":"Legal"},{"n":55,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/info17040389","authors":"Jovanovski, D.; Stojcheva, M.; Dodevska, M.; ... Gjorgjevikj, D.","year":"2026","journal":"Information (Switzerland)","title":"An Empirical Study of Knowledge Graph-Enhanced RAG for Information Security Compliance","abstract":"Information security compliance has become critical for organizations worldwide, with the ISO/IEC 27000 family serving as the most widely adopted framework for establishing information security management systems. Despite their global acceptance, these standards present significant interpretation challenges due to their formal language, abstract structure, and extensive cross-referencing across 97 documents. Traditional retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems, which rely on independent text chunking and dense vector retrieval, prove inadequate for such highly interconnected regulatory materials, often fragmenting contextual relationships and reducing accuracy. This study introduces a privacy-preserving RAG framework that integrates LightRAG, a knowledge graph-based retrieval system, with locally hosted open-source language models. Unlike chunk-based RAG systems that treat document segments independently, the system in this study constructs a semantic knowledge graph that explicitly models relationships between clauses through typed edges representing cross-references, semantic similarity, and hierarchical dependencies. To enable rigorous evaluation, we developed a curated benchmark dataset of 222 multiple-choice questions with authoritative ground-truth answers, systematically constructed from official ISO standards, certification preparation materials, and academic sources. Through systematic evaluation on this benchmark, we show that knowledge graph-based retrieval achieves higher accuracy than chunk-based RAG and non-retrieval LLM baselines within the evaluated setup. The analysis indicates that embedding model quality is strongly associated with system performance, that hybrid retrieval modes combining local and global graph traversal tend to yield better accuracy, and that mid-sized open-source models paired with strong retrievers can approach the performance of larger proprietary systems. The best configuration achieves 90.54% accuracy, demonstrating the promising effectiveness of graph-structured retrieval for multiple-choice regulatory questions.","methods":"Empirical evaluation of a privacy-preserving RAG framework using LightRAG and locally hosted open-source language models. A curated benchmark of 222 multiple-choice questions was built from ISO standards, certification materials, and academic sources, with performance compared against chunk-based RAG and non-retrieval LLM baselines.","results":"Knowledge graph-based retrieval outperformed chunk-based RAG and non-retrieval LLM baselines. The best configuration reached 90.54% accuracy; stronger embedding models and hybrid local-global graph traversal improved performance.","conclusions":"Graph-structured retrieval is effective for multiple-choice regulatory questions and can approach larger proprietary systems when paired with strong retrievers. The authors note that performance depends strongly on embedding quality.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"La recuperación con grafos de conocimiento mejora respuestas de cumplimiento, pero depende de la calidad de los embeddings.","tag":"Tecnología"},{"n":56,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050140","authors":"Iovanovici, A.; Prodan, L.","year":"2026","journal":"Journal of Cybersecurity and Privacy","title":"A Structured NIS2–ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Alignment Framework for Higher Education Institutions","abstract":"Higher education institutions operate complex digital environments that combine administrative services, research infrastructures, learning platforms, identity systems, and heterogeneous departmental IT. In the European Union, the NIS2 Directive increases the need for structured cybersecurity governance, while ISO/IEC 27001:2022 provides a mature information security management system standard that can support implementation. This paper proposes a design science artefact for aligning NIS2 obligations with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 clauses and Annex A controls in the context of higher education institutions. The framework organizes cybersecurity governance, asset and service scoping, risk management, incident handling, business continuity, supplier and cloud dependencies, access control, awareness, monitoring, and continual improvement into a staged maturity model. The artefact is instantiated for a Romanian public university context and assessed through internal traceability analysis, including mappings between NIS2 Articles 20, 21, and 23, Romanian NIS2 transposition requirements, and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 control areas. The institutional illustration identifies candidate assessment domains and evidence requirements but does not assign maturity levels because the internal records required by the scoring protocol were unavailable; it therefore does not constitute an audit, verified institutional measurement, or empirical validation. The contribution is therefore a structured and reusable compliance design artefact, together with a transparent mapping method that can support future expert validation, institutional pilots, and audit-oriented refinement.","methods":"Design science artefact proposing an NIS2–ISO/IEC 27001:2022 alignment framework for higher education institutions, instantiated for a Romanian public university and assessed by internal traceability analysis of article-to-control mappings.","results":"The framework organizes governance, scoping, risk, incident handling, continuity, supplier/cloud dependencies, access control, awareness, monitoring, and continual improvement into a staged maturity model. The illustration identified candidate assessment domains and evidence requirements, with mappings between NIS2 Articles 20, 21, and 23, Romanian transposition requirements, and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 control areas.","conclusions":"The paper presents a structured, reusable compliance design artefact and a transparent mapping method for future validation and audit-oriented refinement. No maturity levels were assigned because required internal records were unavailable, so the work is not empirical validation or an audit.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"Un mapeo estructurado NIS2–ISO 27001:2022 facilita auditoría y cumplimiento; requiere registros internos.","tag":"Legal"},{"n":57,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s22239338","authors":"Aslam, M.; Khan Abbasi, M.A.; Khalid, T.; ... Ahmad, R.","year":"2022","journal":"Sensors","title":"Getting Smarter about Smart Cities: Improving Data Security and Privacy through Compliance","abstract":"Smart cities assure the masses a higher quality of life through digital interconnectivity, leading to increased efficiency and accessibility in cities. In addition, a huge amount of data is being exchanged through smart devices, networks, cloud infrastructure, big data analysis and Internet of Things (IoT) applications in the various private and public sectors, such as critical infrastructures, financial sectors, healthcare, and Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs). However, these sectors require maintaining certain security mechanisms to ensure the confidentiality and integrity of personal and critical information. However, unfortunately, organizations fail to maintain their security posture in terms of security mechanisms and controls, which leads to data breach incidents either intentionally or inadvertently due to the vulnerabilities in their information management systems that either malicious insiders or attackers exploit. In this paper, we highlight the importance of data breaches and issues related to information leakage incidents. In particular, the impact of data breaching incidents and the reasons contributing to such incidents affect the citizens’ well-being. In addition, this paper also discusses various preventive measures such as security mechanisms, laws, standards, procedures, and best practices, including follow-up mitigation strategies.","methods":"Narrative paper on smart city data security and privacy; no formal study design or search method is described in the abstract.","results":"Highlights that smart-city data exchange creates security and privacy risks, with organizations often failing to maintain adequate controls, leading to data breaches and information leakage.","conclusions":"Security mechanisms, laws, standards, procedures, best practices, and mitigation strategies are needed to reduce breaches and protect confidential information. The abstract does not state specific limitations.","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","open":true,"insight":"Proteger datos requiere combinar leyes, estándares, procedimientos y mitigaciones técnicas.","tag":"Legal"}];
